window.harnessChunks["rows-c1deac8d2bffabfb.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:32:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/taikkyithar/travis234\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Travis234</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/taikkyithar/travis234/tree/97aff8b174fe95064b390f30da07093b33de3c0b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 97aff8b174fe95064b390f30da07093b33de3c0b\">97aff8b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Act on uncertain local coding tasks by selecting tool operations and integrating observed results before subsequent decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository content, code failures, shell/process results, context growth, provider-specific tool format and interrupt/cancellation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The active root coding model autonomously chooses read/search/edit/shell/process actions and terminal response from real returned tool data; an explicitly delegated child model likewise owns its scoped task. - Decision owner: Native model-driven Agent loop within Travis234 runtime; provider adapters transport inference, while local tool/session code executes and reports action feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed coding tool catalog, permission/project-trust barriers, process/tmux ownership, session persistence/compaction, token budgeting and tool interruption. - Closure path: Developer intent → model-selected tool call → first-party tool/process executor → normalized tool result persisted in the agent session → model chooses correction/next call/final answer. - Boundary reachability: Standard installed travis234 CLI/TUI and print modes instantiate the native coding AgentSession/runtime; the packaged agent loop connects registered coding tools to the configured provider. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic tool schemas, trust gates and session storage cannot select situational coding actions without the model&#39;s task-based judgment. - Evidence: agentloop.py; AgentSession; session runtime; agent harness; runtime overview. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No fully function-specific first-party regulation of interference/oscillation among independent child S1 workers with returned correction is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel children editing overlapping files can collide; product guidance demands disjoint file ownership, but no native collision adjudicator dynamically returns coordination decisions to competing S1s. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Parent assigns bounded, supposedly disjoint tasks; static per-turn spawn limits and duplicate-role-and-goal suppression prevent redundant invocations but do not inspect or resolve overlapping file commitments. - Decision owner: Human/root coding agent for delegation constraints and task decomposition; deterministic runtime for spawn quotas and cancellation. No S2-specific conflict arbitration owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubagentSupervisor, capped worker pool, session-owned child tasks, instruction to use disjoint files, duplicate spawn blocking, process lifecycle cleanup. - Closure path: Child result/status returns to the parent; no native observed edit-ownership collision → coordination choice → corrected obligations sent to both clashing child S1s. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Instructions to avoid contention and a list of tasks are not themselves a closed inter-S1 conflict attenuation mechanism. - Evidence: sessionsubagents.py; SubagentSupervisor; delegation skill; subagent boundaries. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No independent whole-current organizational management function spanning sustained operational units with autonomous or parent-governed corrective authority is proven. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Subagent tasks, running processes and token/context pressure are task-local to a coding session; transient child lifecycle is not sufficient to show whole-current resource bargaining/accountability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Root coding model may delegate and interpret child results, while runtime cancels/limits active child tasks and users control sessions. No distinct whole-system current-manager feedback loop is established. - Decision owner: Parent coder for its own user task, operator for manual cancellation and backend for deterministic restrictions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubagentSupervisor status/wait/cancel, tool visibility in explicitly delegated turns, process owner registry, session lifecycle and compaction controls. - Closure path: Delegate → child returns summary → root resumes local task. Missing whole-current portfolio management view → independent resource/priority decision → returned organizational commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Delegating one coding job to bounded children, listing them and stopping them is nested task execution rather than separate S3 current control. - Evidence: supervisor; delegation binding; turn lifecycle; process/subagent runtime. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: In an explicitly requested independent reviewer mode, have a separate child model inspect repository artifacts directly and return evidentiary judgments that can challenge the root coding agent&#39;s normal view. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unverified claims about produced changes or repository behavior and the risk that the same coding model merely self-reports completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independent reviewer child model chooses actual read/grep/find/bash/inspection actions and reaches a grounded judgment. The parent receives bounded findings/evidence and can correct or reject produced work. - Decision owner: Distinct first-party child Agent execution owns the audit findings in the explicit reviewer/delegation mode, not the producer agent&#39;s self-report or deterministic result formatter. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Explicit independent-review intent gate, spawnsubagent reviewer role, child-owned isolated model turn and tools, no-parent-preinspection instructions, trace/artifact/evidence return contract and bounded result expansion. - Closure path: User explicitly requests independent review of a target → child reviewer receives only exact scoped paths/goal in isolated context → directly investigates source/changed files with its own tools → structured findings returned by wait/getsubagentresult → parent agent can reconsider or act on the finding. - Boundary reachability: Explicit reviewer/subagent requests activate the first-party spawnsubagent and result-return tools in AgentSession; the packaged delegation contract requires child-origin repository inspection and separate evidentiary summary, which is a supported opt-in standard mode rather than an unconnected example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer model makes the complementary evidentiary judgment; tool restriction and result collection only enforce/transport it. Independent review is a supported opt-in standard-distribution mode, not asserted automatic for all coding turns. - Claim being audited: The coding session&#39;s account of changes, file contents, code behavior or claimed task outcome. - Ordinary reporting path: Main coding Agent tool thread, parent/worker summaries and claimed completion. - Complementary access path: Dedicated subagent directly reads or tests original repository targets with read/grep/find/bash/tools rather than consuming only the parent&#39;s narrative. - Independence boundary: Child has separate bounded context/session; shipped delegation contract forbids parent pre-reading delegated targets and requires child evidence. This is first-party instruction-level separation, not a cryptographically enforced producer-blind review. - Who acts on findings: Parent coding agent receives child status, confirmed findings, artifacts, blockers and subsequent corrective action is selected in its ordinary tool loop. - Evidence: reviewer subagent tools and result path; independent child contract and supervisor; no-parent-preinspection and child evidence contract; explicit independent-review mode; subagent test surface. - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: medium for opt-in independent evidence-gathering mode; outcome correctness and actual blindness not empirically established - Caveats: Not credited as automatic organization-wide audit or immutable review gate; the separate reviewer must be genuinely directed to inspect evidence, and its findings are not a guaranteed correct verdict. An explicit user prohibition disables this mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous external-and-prospective intelligence function with a decision path changing sustained organizational capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider/model availability, extension ecosystem, user requests and context limitations can change; native provider model selection and extension loading operate present capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User chooses provider, installs/extensions or requests extensions; model may help write one on request but no independent horizon-sensing, future-option selection or S4-to-current-control return is wired. - Decision owner: Operator for installed capabilities and model switching, active model for in-task code generation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider control plane, extension loaders/reload, skills and resource hierarchy, context compaction, package installer, model catalog. - Closure path: External/package settings → operator-driven selection or setup → next coding session tools; no prospective environment scan and capability-adaptation judgment loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Current model selection, context compression or capability extension when prompted does not constitute outside-and-then intelligence by itself. - Evidence: model registry; extension runtime; package controls; model/extension modes. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party legitimate ultimate purpose/identity authority deciding an organization-level policy matter and returning it into subsequent operations is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Workspace trust, credential isolation, project resource permissions and session policies constrain execution but do not arbitrate ultimate identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human user approves trusted workspace and model/provider use; runtime enforces policy and tool bounds. Model does not resolve constitutional identity-level disputes. - Decision owner: Operator for trust/safety configuration, runtime for deterministic enforcement; no explicit S5 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project-trust system, isolated unprivileged sandbox, tool permissions, provider-specific secrets, session policy controller and runtime flags. - Closure path: Local trust/user approval → permission gate / constrained tool execution. Missing ultimate-policy issue → legitimate S5 authority → binding organizational return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A safety/permission gate and product identity text are operational constraints rather than ultimate authority over continuing organizational identity. - Evidence: workspace trust; session policy; security boundary. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7e99ace806401b461566aa2720cffa06be65868f/assessments/travis234.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7e99ace806401b461566aa2720cffa06be65868f\">7e99ace &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T17:07:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hsghcop/ForestCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ForestCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hsghcop/ForestCode/tree/406c792be7d365878113a70d2f1feff7aef94e1e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 406c792be7d365878113a70d2f1feff7aef94e1e\">406c792 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: investigate and modify repository artifacts in response to user goals and tool observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: file contents, command results, rejected actions and coding errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next tool/arguments or final response. - Decision owner: model-backed parent and configured operational child agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: context builder, model router, tool executor, patch service, permission checks, session/memory store and stop policy. - Closure path: model response becomes executed calls; results enter RunState and the next context/model request. - Boundary reachability: the installed CLI reaches the terminal bridge&#39;s supplied AgentLoop/factory, including child model construction without an external agent runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model removal retains enforcement/storage but removes the discretionary repository action strategy. - Evidence: Loop and returned tools, Independent child construction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source availability, not provider performance; mutations remain approval-gated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent concurrent parent/child writers from computing and applying mutually stale file changes or overlapping mutation approvals. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two coding cells propose edits against shared workspace state and a later writer silently clobbers an earlier result. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define single-writer mutation ordering and reject a stale apply; broader discretionary collision-resolution policy remains a composition responsibility. - Decision owner: developer-selected first-party coordination policy, enforced by a shared runtime gate; no autonomous coordination actor is established for this policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reentrant shared lock across propose → approval → apply/execute and PatchService content-hash stale-write checks. - Closure path: later mutation sections wait before proposal; stale writes fail instead of overwriting and their tool result returns to the agent for subsequent action. - Boundary reachability: terminal bridge creates one MutationGate for the run and passes it into parent runtime and child factory; ToolExecutor actually acquires it around the complete mutation section. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents choose coding actions but the serialization response is selected in implementation; removing any putative coordinator model leaves the same gate policy. - Distinct S1 units: parent coder and concurrent configured edit/full child coders. - Inter-S1 disturbance: same-workspace competing mutation proposals and stale write application. - Attenuating coordination relation: one shared mutation critical section plus content-hash rejection. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: contenders wait for an up-to-date proposal/approval section or receive a failed stale apply, changing the subsequent edit path. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the code explicitly identifies multi-writer clobbering and regulates the actual shared mutation, independently of FIFO task allocation. - Evidence: Collision-specific gate, Shared parent/child wiring, Child gate attachment, Mutation execution. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: C is a supplied coordination construction path, not an agent-owned coordination policy or proof of semantic conflict resolution.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the parent run&#39;s active/queued child commitments through live status and selective intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: work no longer required, failed/pending child commitments, blocked approvals and the bounded shared execution capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the parent model can inspect all current child snapshots/results, cancel a selected queued/running child and choose subsequent delegation/waiting. - Decision owner: the parent model agent for discretionary intervention; fixed pool size and watchdog timeouts are developer/runtime enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordinator task snapshots, list/wait/cancel tool wrappers, abort signal, per-child approval-ticket cancellation, FIFO queue and worker lifecycle. - Closure path: selected queued work is removed immediately; a running/waiting child receives abort, late results are discarded, and cancellation/status returns through the model-visible tools. Actual worker exit governs slot release before new work proceeds. - Boundary reachability: terminal bridge wires the coordinator and exposes its model tools in the same parent run; the ordinary model/tool loop receives their returned snapshots. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime cannot choose which otherwise valid child commitment the whole run should abandon; the model chooses that intervention and the scheduler enforces it. - Whole-system current view: all child tasks of this parent run, queue positions, status, results/errors and approval-wait/cancel state. - Current-control decision scope: continue or curtail the run&#39;s current subordinate commitments; not just initial task decomposition, and not autonomous selection of maxworkers or ultimate policy. - Evidence: Model-visible view and intervention, Actual cancellation effects, Tool wiring, Completion/cancellation return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: narrow run-relative control, cooperative cancellation and bounded telemetry; not a claim of hard process kill, resource optimization or parent-human S3 governance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for this function at the declared run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Separate child contexts hide parent/sibling transcripts and the verify profile can expose command execution, but no built-in auditor is assigned an independent claim-challenging relation with mandatory or optional audit return as such. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for the specific higher function. - Decision owner: not established; operational model/developer/operator rights are kept separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic tools, configuration, persistence and event transport do not inherit this function. - Closure path: operational result feedback exists, without the distinct function-specific loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: absence concerns the organizational relation before ownership grading. - Evidence: src/forestcode/subagents/types.py, src/forestcode/runtime/factory.py, src/forestcode/subagents/tools.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: bounded absence, not a claim that an external/custom organization could never add the function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for this function at the declared run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-term memory, session summarization, skills and provider overrides assemble/recover current context. They do not by themselves produce prospective adaptation options from external environmental distinctions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for the specific higher function. - Decision owner: not established; operational model/developer/operator rights are kept separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic tools, configuration, persistence and event transport do not inherit this function. - Closure path: operational result feedback exists, without the distinct function-specific loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: absence concerns the organizational relation before ownership grading. - Evidence: READMEEN.md, src/forestcode/core/agentloop.py, src/forestcode/runtime/factory.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: bounded absence, not a claim that an external/custom organization could never add the function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for this function at the declared run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permissions, tool visibility and confirmation requests bound coding actions. Parent cancellation controls current work, while user approval authorizes an individual edit/command; neither is an identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for the specific higher function. - Decision owner: not established; operational model/developer/operator rights are kept separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic tools, configuration, persistence and event transport do not inherit this function. - Closure path: operational result feedback exists, without the distinct function-specific loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: absence concerns the organizational relation before ownership grading. - Evidence: READMEEN.md, src/forestcode/subagents/types.py, src/forestcode/runtime/factory.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: bounded absence, not a claim that an external/custom organization could never add the function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3df4fa68265a00adfef1808c9b2f6774e337e6d9/assessments/fo/forestcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3df4fa68265a00adfef1808c9b2f6774e337e6d9\">3df4fa6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T15:23:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DeepExperience/Harness-R1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness-R1</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DeepExperience/Harness-R1/tree/2b3f125710ed14ea8a863e6b39a2eb9951cc7181\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2b3f125710ed14ea8a863e6b39a2eb9951cc7181\">2b3f125 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Environment-coupled agent task action and outcome production. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Variable task goals, search/click affordances, observations and failed tool outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured target model selects tool call and subsequent response in light of real prior environment outcomes. - Decision owner: Model-backed target agent instantiated into the first-party AgentBench task loop; third-party inference is a service, not the harness governance owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session state, WebShop tools/environment, hook/guard runtime, tool schema, step budget and output collection. - Closure path: Environment observation → session.syncaction → model toolcalls → first-party tool/environment effect → observation delivered to the next session turn → task result. - Boundary reachability: The first-party task code actually invokes the decision loop via its shipped evaluation task definition and runnable entrypoint, not merely an optional abstract integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model discretion leaves fixed rules and replay but not novel choice over next action; restrictions constrain rather than originate context-sensitive tool decisions. - Evidence: task.py L469-550; task.py L542-655; harnessr1batchdebug.py L483-496. - Basis: Explicit source-wired operational agent/tool feedback. - Confidence: High structural confidence; live hosted inference not run. - Caveats: Hard-coded hooks may force or block local actions; benchmark success is not established by this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated first-party disturbance-specific reciprocal regulation of independently viable S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent evaluation batches and processes risk shared ports/output collision, handled by static per-batch allocation and isolation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runner assigns deterministic worker port, batch order and process lifecycle rather than exercising context-sensitive inter-S1 coordination authority. - Decision owner: External run configuration and deterministic evaluator, not an agent-owned S2-specific controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Batch-specific port/paths, worker process launch, configured limits, isolation. - Closure path: Scheduled subprocesses produce isolated run outputs; no observed inter-S1 disturbance → revised mutual operating constraint → feedback into distinct S1 units is present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concurrency protection and sequencing alone are not a VSM S2 coordination relation between independent operational cells. - Evidence: harnessr1batchdebug.py L1-100; harnessr1batchdebug.py L560-680; evalwebshopcheckpointpatches.py L462-625. - Basis: Bounded first-party negative after inspecting parallel-run paths. - Confidence: Medium-high within shipped experiment mode. - Caveats: External orchestrators may build separate coordination that is outside the reviewed recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No agent-owned or operationally closed parent-governed whole-current resource/commitment regulation at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task execution failures, patch rejection and timeouts threaten single experiment batch execution, not a standing whole-current portfolio. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Fixed CLI/config run bounds and target assignment, not a whole-system priority/commitment intervention decision. - Decision owner: Experiment operator and deterministic runner for ordinary task scheduling; engineer model owns a prospective patch, not current multi-unit resource bargaining. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worker concurrency limits, retry/failure statuses, task metrics, process cleanup. - Closure path: Batch failures → ordinary retry/score; missing whole-current resource/commitment view → S3 choice → binding cross-unit current action. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Executing budgets, task dispatch or scoring does not confer independent current-control decision rights. - Evidence: evalwebshopcheckpointpatches.py L462-625; evalwebshopcheckpointpatches.py L626-815; harnessr1batchdebug.py L560-680. - Basis: Bounded absence for first-party real experiment execution. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An external researcher may revise the larger experimental plan, which is not a supported first-party S3 parent closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Ordinary evaluation of an edited harness is not a complementary independent audit of a separate S3 management claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfair patch comparisons and invalid claimed patch effects are checked against fresh task identities and matched rewards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Evaluator calculates deterministic success/reward and validates task identity; no independent corrective audit actor owns a challenge to ordinary S3 reporting. - Decision owner: Patch quality/evaluation pipeline, with external environment providing ground-truth task reward; not an independent S3 mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task manifest hashes, evaluation logs, score delta, restricted code validator. - Closure path: Patch → fresh paired target task rerun → reward delta → engineer feedback; no independent audit finding → accountable S3 correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A legitimate experimental patch test is not automatically independently commissioned whole-current management audit. - Evidence: evalwebshopcheckpointpatches.py L462-625; rewardwebshoppatch.py L989-1004; coderunner.py L136-225. - Basis: Negative at declared experiment recursion, after testing complementary-report candidates. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Quality validation is genuine first-party functionality, but a different organizational function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Prospectively change the executable target harness from observed external-task failures and return this generated option into later agent capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task-environment failures and varying external action affordances exceed the previous fixed guidance/guard strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured model-backed engineer generates a new executable code hook from failure trajectories; the first-party patch validator accepts its syntax/effect bounds, and the evaluator installs it for a subsequent operating episode. - Decision owner: Harness engineer agent in the shipped evaluation workflow chooses adaptation content; first-party code enforces patch restrictions and installs accepted proposal. External model endpoint supplies inference and does not own organizational policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Failure packet, patch schema, AST sandbox, recorded patch path, task-definition overlay, matched identity check, reward delta and persisted artifacts. - Closure path: Previous target failures → engineer model call → code-hook proposal → normalize/compile → patch.json → patched new target run → actual changed guidance/action effect → fresh reward/observation feedback. - Boundary reachability: The shipped evaluation entrypoint invokes the engineer and target runner; the runner installs generated overlay into the WebShop task where the hooks execute in new environment sessions. The path is not only a paper claim or an offline stored-demo. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the engineer model, validators can only check/replay supplied code; source-grounded adaptation-option judgment depends on model output rather than deterministic enforcement. - External distinction: New WebShop/ALFWorld/DBBench environment failure patterns and actual action trajectories, not merely an internal instruction. - Future / prospective distinction: Earlier observed failure batch informs code installed into a subsequent target episode; held-out evaluation documents additional future task applications but reported numerical benefits are not accepted as independent result evidence. - Adaptation option generated: Executable source code for oninit, makeprehint, onbeforeaction and/or onpoststep hooks in the runtime substrate. - Path back into current capability / S3: Generated patch → compilepatchtotaskdefinition → live harnessoverlay → later tool-coupled task operation → measured feedback. Closure returns directly into changed S1 capability; no separate S3 is inferred. - Evidence: evalwebshopcheckpointpatches.py L462-625; evalwebshopcheckpointpatches.py L626-815; harnessr1patch.py L357-443; harnessr1patch.py L669-715; harnessr1batchdebug.py L483-496; task.py L252-295; task.py L469-550; README.md L1-25. - Basis: Source-wired model-proposed option, validated hook installation, next-run operational effect and experimental feedback. - Confidence: Medium-high for bounded experimental S4, not deployed autonomy. - Caveats: Same-batch rerun is a controlled prospective test, not automatic universal promotion. A generated-only file, a stored demo and offline RL weight training alone would not close S4. No online model experiment was run by this assessor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate-purpose/identity arbitration with returned authority is supplied by the shipped experiment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Patch safety, benchmark identity and static experiment constraints bound action, but not conflicts over ultimate mission or ethos. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Fixed task/patch configurations and sandbox enforce preset limits; no owner arbitrates an identity-level matter at runtime. - Decision owner: Developer/operator externally chooses experiments; no first-party S5 agent or legitimate parent-governed runtime return path is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Restricted patch schema, sandbox limits, CLI configuration and release licensing. - Closure path: Static rules → allowed/disallowed patches; absent identity/policy issue → legitimate final authority → returned binding operational policy revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Restrictions do not originate autonomous ultimate policy; human launching a benchmark is not a parent S5 organization decision. - Evidence: harnessr1patch.py L357-443; coderunner.py L136-225; evalwebshop.sh L1-75. - Basis: Bounded absence after examining identity and policy candidates. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Real research governance could exist outside these first-party executable surfaces and cannot be silently included.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/99455c58fb83d5af531d405468e2fc1e36d3e5b6/assessments/harness-r1.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 99455c58fb83d5af531d405468e2fc1e36d3e5b6\">99455c5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T15:50:49Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QwenAudio/qwen-audio-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Qwen Audio Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QwenAudio/qwen-audio-agent/tree/7b5913a05666a6cab9681c301807206ffa66c106\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7b5913a05666a6cab9681c301807206ffa66c106\">7b5913a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: conduct realtime user-facing assistance by interpreting current conversation/context, choosing a substantive response or available frontend action, and incorporating returned tool/task results into later responses. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user intent, conversational context, available capabilities, tool/retrieval results, task status/results, pending clarification/permission state, attachments and persistent personalization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to answer directly, invoke an available specialized frontend tool, submit sustained work through spawnthinking, ask a necessary question, or incorporate a returned result into the next response. - Decision owner: the configured Realtime model acting as the frontend agent under the shipped first-party prompt/tool contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Gateway Realtime transport, frontend tool registry, capability gating, task/status/permission envelopes, conversation/session persistence, memory context and deterministic tool execution. - Closure path: user/context input → Realtime model selects response/tool action → first-party Gateway executes or routes the action → result/state returns into model context → later model response/action. - Boundary reachability: the Realtime frontend and tool registry are the standard shipped user-facing runtime and remain operational even in frontend-only mode; no backend-private agent or repository-development actor is needed for this S1 path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model discretion while retaining the Gateway/tool machinery eliminates the substantive choice among direct response, tool use and delegation; deterministic runtime code validates, routes and executes the selected action rather than making an equivalent conversational/task decision itself. - Evidence: PROMPT.md; frontend-tools.mjs; deep-dive.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model-provider cognition is outside the repository implementation boundary; the credited autonomy is the supported first-party model/context/tool feedback loop around that agent actor. Backend Agent work is not needed to establish S1 and its internal autonomy is not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party coordination function was established that attenuates a specific interference/oscillation among distinct credited S1 operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate S2 disturbance would be destructive interference among multiple autonomous operational units; the reviewed first-party product boundary instead exposes one credited Realtime S1 and one optionally integrated external backend system. - Distinct S1 units: multiple Task records, queued requests and backend Sessions are not automatically S1 units. Backend-private subagents/Sessions belong to the configured external backend unless independently brought inside the declared boundary. - Inter-S1 disturbance: no specific first-party disturbance among two or more credited qwen-audio-agent S1 units was established. - Attenuating coordination relation: owner FIFO scheduling, backend write serialization, task claims and delivery deduplication regulate runtime races and lifecycle ordering but are generic infrastructure mechanisms rather than an established inter-S1 coordination relation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: no function-specific S2 mutual-adjustment closure was established. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the repository explicitly uses queues, Session correlation and delegation to move/order work; Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat those surfaces as S2 without distinct S1 units plus a concrete cross-unit interference witness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskScheduler, per-owner FIFO queue, backend serialization lock, renewable delivery claims, Task registry and delegation correlation. - Closure path: not established for an S2 function. - Boundary reachability: not applicable because no positive S2 function is published. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the frontend can delegate work and the external backend can create private Sessions, but task decomposition/routing does not by itself regulate a specific disturbance among qwen-audio-agent operational units. - Evidence: deep-dive.md; task-manager.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a configured backend may internally contain multiple coordinated agents; those backend-private arrangements are not first-party qwen-audio-agent S2 evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-control function was established over multiple operational units&#39; shared resources, commitments, priorities, accountability or synergy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the Gateway tracks task lifecycle, capacity and permissions, but these do not establish discretionary whole-system regulation of current operations at the assessed recursion. - Whole-system current view: TaskManager can enumerate and persist current Tasks, and the Realtime frontend can query task status, but the architecture intentionally withholds backend Session topology, execution strategy, tools, Agents and subagents from the frontend control surface. - Current-control decision scope: users/models can query or cancel a selected Task and relay explicit permission/input responses; static schedulers enforce capacity. No first-party actor is shown bargaining/revising whole-system resources, priorities or commitments across autonomous operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskManager, TaskScheduler, cancellation state machine, permission policy, progress/status projection, backend locks and restart recovery. - Closure path: no S3-level whole-system control loop established. - Boundary reachability: not applicable because no positive S3 function is published. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Realtime model can request status/cancellation but explicitly cannot select/create/continue/cancel backend Sessions, choose execution mode/strategy, or select backend tools/Agents/subagents. Deterministic Gateway controls enforce lifecycle rather than exercise organizational S3 discretion. - Evidence: deep-dive.md; PROMPT.md; task-manager.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: current-task cancellation and permission approval are real control actions but remain below the S3 threshold because they do not reconstruct a whole-system current-control function at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path was established that challenges ordinary operational claims and returns findings into subsequent control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate S3 disturbance would be uncertainty that ordinary task/model reporting matches actual operational reality; first-party validation focuses on normal lifecycle integrity rather than a separate complementary audit function. - Claim being audited: Task completion, delegation correlation, permissions, memory-learning evidence and delivery state all receive deterministic validation, but these are ordinary execution/data-integrity claims in their primary paths. - Ordinary reporting path: backend protocol events, Gateway Task records, frontend tool results, model/user conversation and memory-learning observations. - Complementary access path: not established. Exact delegation/result correlation, stopReason completion gating, audit logs and structural preference guards inspect the same operational path or provide diagnostics rather than an independent alternative view of reality. - Independence boundary: no first-party auditor with materially different access from the producing operational path was found in the credited distribution. - Who acts on findings: routine runtime code rejects invalid/stale lifecycle or learning inputs; diagnostic audit records can be inspected by an operator, but no complementary-audit finding loop into S3 is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegation correlation, completion-state validation, memory-audit log, sensitive-content/evidence guards, delivery claims and protocol validation. - Closure path: no complementary S3 closure established. - Boundary reachability: not applicable because no positive S3 function is published. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model- or runtime-based checks inside the normal production/learning path do not become S3 merely because they reject bad state; materially independent access and audit feedback are missing. - Evidence: deep-dive.md; profile-observer.mjs; preference-learning.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository tests and scenario validation are adjacent development/evaluation surfaces and are not borrowed as runtime S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: adapt future assistant behaviour to stable user characteristics and long-term interaction preferences learned or explicitly selected across sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user characteristics and preferred interaction style can remain unknown, change, or differ from the instance-wide default; blindly carrying one fixed persona/reply strategy across future sessions would lose environment-relevant variety. - External distinction: the evidence comes from the external user/environment — user utterances and explicit preference settings — rather than from internal task-planning state alone. - Future / prospective distinction: accepted observations/preferences are persisted specifically to influence later interactions; inferred promotions are not injected back into the just-ended session, and direct profile edits are documented to apply to subsequent voice sessions. - Adaptation option generated: in autonomous mode, ProfileObserver uses a text-model call to infer bounded candidate values for occupation, special skills, response length and response style from actual user utterances; candidates are cross-session-confirmed and promoted into the observed section of USER.md. In parent mode, the user/operator explicitly selects a durable personalization change such as preferred name/language/reply style/default behavior. - Path back into current capability / S3: promoted or explicit USER.md content is composed into later frontend instructions as &lt;userpreferences&gt; and has higher personalization precedence than the default assistant profile, changing later Realtime model behaviour; explicit tool writes can update the active preference context while direct file edits apply on later sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — the observer model makes the substantive semantic inference from user evidence, while deterministic quote/field guards and multi-session promotion thresholds validate/enforce when that option may become durable. Parent mode — the legitimate user/operator directly decides the long-term personalization setting/correction and first-party memory machinery returns it into future frontend context. - Decision owner: base mode — model-driven ProfileObserver; parent mode — current user/operator for their durable personalization overlay. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: candidate pool/store, evidence anchoring guards, ≥2-confirmation/≥2-session promotion gate, PreferencePromoter, owner-serialized memory writes, USER.md, memory provider/runtime and frontend context composition. - Closure path: completed sessions → model observer infers candidate from user evidence → candidate accumulates independent cross-session confirmations → promoter persists accepted preference → later frontend context loads preference → subsequent agent responses operate under the adapted preference. Parent mode: explicit durable preference/correction → memory tool/API/direct edit → persisted USER.md → subsequent frontend context/behaviour. - Boundary reachability: both paths are first-party documented product modes at the frozen revision. Autonomous preference learning is optional/off by default but enabled through the supported QWENAUDIOPREFERENCELEARNING=on configuration; explicit long-term personalization is part of the standard conversational/product interface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in the autonomous mode, removing the observer model while leaving storage, guards and thresholds intact eliminates the substantive inference that proposes what environmental/user distinction should change future behaviour; deterministic machinery validates and promotes that model-generated option rather than inventing an equivalent preference itself. The distinct parent mode intentionally gives that adaptation judgment to the user/operator. - Evidence: preference-learning.md; personalization.md; profile-observer.mjs; preference-promoter.mjs; session-observer.mjs; module.mjs; frontend-tools.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: autonomous learning is deliberately narrow, optional and limited to four user-trait fields; this state does not claim general self-modification, model training, tool evolution or strategic environmental research.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: maintain the assistant instance&#39;s default identity/persona at the assessed product recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the deployment may require a different stable assistant name, personality, relationship stance or expression style while preserving one coherent instance-wide default across sessions and users&#39; overlays. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what default assistant identity/personality/relationship stance/expression style this deployed qwen-audio-agent instance presents when not superseded by a legitimate per-user personalization override. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the deployment user/operator controlling the local ASSISTANT.md (or configured assistant-profile path) is the parent authority for this identity surface. No autonomous first-party S5 owner is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: first launch materializes the packaged assistant-profile template; the parent edits the local profile; qwen-audio-agent preserves it across upgrades and loads it into &lt;assistantprofile&gt; for later voice sessions; the first-party instruction hierarchy then makes that profile govern default identity/persona while keeping core tool/permission/safety/task policy non-overridable by persona or memory. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity/persona changes are separated from per-user memory and from core operational policy so a deployment-level identity decision can persist without being confused with task permissions or transient conversation state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the instance-wide default assistant identity/personality/relationship/expression profile that subsequent sessions receive. - Decision owner: parent deployment user/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: local ASSISTANT.md, configurable profile path, template-on-first-launch behavior, upgrade preservation, resolveAssistantProfile/frontend context composition and prompt authority hierarchy. - Closure path: identity/persona decision → parent edits supported local profile → runtime reloads profile on subsequent session → Realtime frontend receives it as authoritative persona-default context → subsequent behaviour reflects returned identity decision. - Boundary reachability: ASSISTANT.md is a documented standard local configuration surface at the frozen revision, not a repository-maintainer/development-only file; the normal frontend instruction builder loads the resolved assistant profile into operating sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime and model consume/enforce the selected profile but do not hold the ultimate right to change the instance-wide profile through the memory/learning path. Documentation explicitly prevents memory from editing ASSISTANT.md; the decisive identity choice remains with the parent operator. - Evidence: personalization.md; deep-dive.md; frontend-tools.mjs; PROMPT.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this S5 credit is intentionally limited to instance identity/persona. Ordinary backend permission approval, task cancellation, static core prompt text and per-user preferences are not used as S5 witnesses.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/96935c61990d83cc34b88a8463b57cdb5beb6295/assessments/qwen-audio-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 96935c61990d83cc34b88a8463b57cdb5beb6295\">96935c6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T00:10:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/t54-labs/agent-commons\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Commons</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-01\">2026-10-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/t54-labs/agent-commons/tree/6e911236127dc6cf0d231add87e302974151a7f4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6e911236127dc6cf0d231add87e302974151a7f4\">6e91123 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing coding, validation, deployment or other engineering work as an enrolled Commons-enabled agent work cell while keeping its declared task/plan/evidence state coupled to the coordination system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific repository and tool state, implementation choices, test/deployment feedback, changing local evidence, blockers and responses from the human or peer agents that affect the cell&#39;s next operational action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise the next task-specific coding/tool/action step from the current objective and returned evidence while respecting Commons coordination constraints. - Decision owner: the supported autonomous coding-agent session (for example Codex, Claude Code or Cline) operating with the shipped Commons Skill in an enrolled workspace. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Commons scope and registration, task/plan records, inbox/messages, lease state, wrappers, durable audit events, local/Relay persistence and the Skill&#39;s lifecycle instructions. - Closure path: enrolled session resolves identity/scope → agent declares task and plan → agent performs task-specific work through its runtime/environment subject to coordination state → repository/tool/environment evidence returns → the same agent revises, continues, completes or escalates and updates Commons state. - Boundary reachability: the verified package carries the canonical Skill and commons install-skill installs it into supported Codex/Claude Code/Cline Skill locations; the shipped Skill then governs enrolled session startup, work and completion without application-authored glue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the autonomous coding-agent actor while retaining Commons tasks, messages, SQLite, leases and Console leaves coordination infrastructure but no entity that makes the open-ended repository-facing operational decisions. Commons does not itself run the model, so the S1 decision right remains with the participating autonomous agent cell rather than with the deterministic control plane. - Evidence: README.md; .agents/skills/commons/SKILL.md; docs/architecture.md; docs/commons-implementation-status.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the positive state applies to the Commons-enabled assembled operating mode. It does not import planning, delegation, memory, policy or audit functions from the external agent runtime itself, and a workspace enrolled as disabled is outside the active operational organization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among independently operating agent cells that intend to mutate the same shared engineering resource, and feed the conflict result back into their subsequent behaviour. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous deploys, migrations, Git writes, browser-profile takeover, server/port use or other overlapping mutations where two agent cells could otherwise act on one shared mutable resource without knowing the other&#39;s current claim. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently started Commons-enabled coding-agent sessions in the same enrolled local/project scope, potentially across different runtimes, repositories or machines. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a concrete shared-resource collision, such as one agent deploying while another begins a staging smoke/migration operation, or multiple agents attempting writes against the same database, branch, browser profile, server or deployment slot. - Attenuating coordination relation: agents publish plans and inspect peer/lease state; write-like/exclusive/maintenance leases provide canonical resource identity, atomic grant/deny, TTL and fencing epochs; direct messages support holder negotiation and handoff; optional wrappers gate Commons-controlled risky commands against the current lease. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a denied lease is returned to the requesting agent; the shipped Skill says not to proceed and instead contact the holder, wait, change the plan or ask the user. Release/handoff or a new fenced grant then permits later operation, so the coordination result materially changes subsequent S1 action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is not merely messages or a shared task board. Commons identifies named classes of real shared-resource interference and supplies a conflict relation specifically designed to prevent stale or simultaneous mutation, plus an explicit behavioral return from grant/denial into later agent action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after Commons exposes the conflict/grant state, the autonomous requesting/holding agents own the discretionary mutual-adjustment response—whether to hand off, wait, revise resource use or escalate—while the deterministic lease engine enforces atomic incompatibility and fencing. - Decision owner: participating autonomous agent cells using the first-party installed Skill; Commons runtime machinery owns grant/deny/fencing enforcement but not the discretionary coordination choice following that feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Relay/local SQLite transactions, canonical resource IDs, lease modes, TTLs, fencing epochs, persisted denial events, heartbeats, task/plan state, direct/broadcast messaging and high-risk command wrappers. - Closure path: agents declare intended shared-resource use → Commons detects/serializes incompatible lease claims → grant or denial is returned → agent adjusts plan/communication/action → holder releases/renews or requester waits/changes course → subsequent resource use reflects the coordinated result. - Boundary reachability: lease acquisition and conflict handling are mandatory paths in the shipped Skill for enrolled sessions; the current implementation includes real local/Relay lease engines and wrappers, so no developer-authored coordinator is required to close the supported agent feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the deterministic lease service would still block incompatible grants if the agent were removed, but it would not choose the organizational response to the conflict or perform a peer handoff/replan. The standard Skill supplies the feedback to an already participating autonomous agent, which owns that mutual-adjustment discretion. The runtime enforcement therefore supports rather than replaces agent ownership. - Evidence: README.md; .agents/skills/commons/SKILL.md; docs/architecture.md; docs/why-commons.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Commons cannot prevent an unrelated process from bypassing a lease unless the downstream system uses a Commons wrapper/hook/fencing check. The S2 claim is therefore about the supported Commons-enabled agent organization, not universal exclusion of all out-of-band actors.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-project current-control function is established at the reviewed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential project-wide contention among commitments, priorities, resources and exceptions was checked, but the shipped product does not assign a metasystemic actor authority to resolve that variety on behalf of the whole. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established beyond peer/local task decisions, deterministic resource-conflict enforcement and isolated operator interventions. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at the project recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task ownership/status, agent presence, lease state, policy/wrapper checks, audit history, operator-readable Console and CLI mutation primitives. - Closure path: no project-wide S3 decision-and-return loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: participating agents regulate their own tasks and negotiate shared-resource conflicts, but no agent is granted whole-project authority over current priorities, commitments or resource allocation. Deterministic leases are S2 conflict attenuation rather than S3 discretion. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; docs/commons-product-design.md; docs/commons-implementation-status.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a team can use Commons data as input to human management or build future admin policy, but those adjacent arrangements are not a shipped S3 closure at the frozen ref.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path is established inside the supported Commons operating organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty about whether agent-reported completion/evidence reflects operational reality was inspected. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none supplied by an independent first-party auditor whose finding returns into project control. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only audit events, hash-chain verification, evidence-bearing task updates, artifacts, message receipts, test/runtime-smoke infrastructure and Skill guidance to verify peer claims against primary evidence. - Closure path: no independent audit judgment → control-return path is supplied by Commons itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the ordinary participating agent can inspect repository state or evidence, but that is not materially independent access beyond the operational path. The audit log records what Commons observed; it does not independently challenge an S1 claim. - Evidence: README.md; docs/why-commons.md; docs/commons-implementation-status.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external Git review, tests or an independently run reviewer can supply complementary evidence to a team using Commons, but Commons explicitly says it does not make unverified claims trustworthy and does not package that external acceptance actor as an S3 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party external-and-prospective adaptation function is established for the Commons organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible changes in agent runtimes, team environment, infrastructure, policy and future coordination needs were inspected. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established that converts sensed external/future distinctions into adaptation options and returns them to current capability. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current peer/resource discovery, heartbeat/activity state, scope resolution, audit history, repository roadmap/product documents and runtime compatibility work. - Closure path: no supported outside-and-then adaptation loop is present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: participating agents react to current task/resource conditions; that is present-time operational/S2 regulation, not prospective modelling of the environment and adaptation of Commons capability. - Evidence: README.md; docs/architecture.md; docs/commons-implementation-status.md. - Basis: structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: maintainers have roadmap and compatibility work, but those development/governance surfaces are adjacent to the assessed deployed product organization and are not borrowed as runtime S4 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: close the parent-governed membership/privacy boundary that determines whether a workspace participates in Commons at all and, if so, whether it belongs to local-only or private-Relay coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: accidental or unauthorized enrollment of personal/client/otherwise unrelated workspaces into a coordination network; disclosure of coordination metadata to the wrong scope; ambiguous human attribution for newly registered agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose remote, local or disabled when workspace scope is unknown, and explicitly establish the human owner before an agent can register in an enabled scope. - Decision owner: the legitimate parent human/operator for the workspace. The Skill explicitly forbids inferring the choice or owner and requires an explicit answer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: commons scope resolve, scope enroll, persisted workspace rules/project config, human-owner storage, Relay/project identity, registration checks and the Skill&#39;s prohibition on register/broadcast/inbox/lease activity when scope is disabled or unresolved. - Closure path: session encounters unknown scope → Skill stops and asks parent human → human makes the authoritative participation/scope decision → Commons persists remote, local or disabled enrollment (and human attribution when enabled) → subsequent sessions resolve that decision before registration → enabled sessions join only the selected scope, while disabled sessions perform no Commons coordination operations. - Boundary reachability: scope-first resolution is mandatory in the shipped installed Skill and implemented CLI; it is not a development-only convention or an application-defined callback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the autonomous agent is deliberately denied the ultimate participation choice when scope is unknown. It cannot infer consent or human identity and therefore cannot close this S5 decision internally. The parent decision is returned into runtime behaviour through persisted scope resolution, so the positive mode is P, not a generic human approval annotation. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether this workspace is a member of the Commons coordination organization and which privacy/trust boundary governs its coordination metadata and peer visibility. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent human/operator only; no first-party autonomous S5 mode is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: persisted scope and human attribution are resolved before each new participating session; they govern whether registration and all subsequent Commons coordination operations are allowed and which local/project boundary they use. - Evidence: .agents/skills/commons/SKILL.md; docs/why-commons.md; docs/architecture.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this S5 mapping is boundary-relative. It treats participation/privacy scope as an identity-level membership decision for the selected Commons project/workspace recursion; ordinary lease approval, task status, token possession or resource policy is not promoted to S5.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4f01503055ce286393b5e66f5a3916732a6dc0c0/assessments/commons.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4f01503055ce286393b5e66f5a3916732a6dc0c0\">4f01503 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T15:22:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/solvent-labs-org/metaorcha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Metaorcha</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/solvent-labs-org/metaorcha/tree/8dd8bb23b95339bb32bb2c12d23d03fb94ad1c6d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8dd8bb23b95339bb32bb2c12d23d03fb94ad1c6d\">8dd8bb2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding and other bounded requested work through supported coding-agent/model engines, including several independent implementation desks when an office is configured. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varied coding tasks, source context, failed tool calls, failing tests and alternative implementation approaches. - Decisive decision or feedback right: invoked model agents independently choose task-specific tool calls, code changes and responses to actual results within their configured roles and permits. - Decision owner: each run&#39;s Claude/Codex/Cursor/local-model agent, launched in the first-party MetaBot ask or office execution path; MetaBot supplies execution/gating and provenance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: engine adapters, per-call authorization, worktree/branch isolation, role files, bounded turns/cost, signed receipts and checks. - Closure path: user/office task → a supported model agent receives current project and role → agent reads/edits/executes → real tool/result feedback → model continuation and output on its own task branch. - Boundary reachability: README and CLI expose installed metabot ask and metabot office run, with model engines and real worktree execution; no test-only actor is needed for the claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: source-dependent coding choices are made by model agents; the gate, branch-copy routine and receipt signer cannot themselves decide a bug fix. - Evidence: MetaBot engines and tool operation, office runner, office tests. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: users must configure/sign into engines, supply an office file for multi-desk mode and grant writes where appropriate; CLI local runs are not evidence of remote multi-tenant deployment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct agent-owned inter-S1 disturbance-regulation and return loop established for concurrently active desks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: several desks can produce conflicting alternatives against the same repository, but the shipped response is separate worktrees/branches and later comparison or parent selection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material shipped agent authority to sense competing ongoing desk interference and return an attenuation decision to adjust those desks&#39; subsequent operation. - Decision owner: predefined relay/fan topology, isolated branches and final operator selection; individual advisors inspect completed work rather than coordinate simultaneous interference. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: desk-specific worktrees, capped fan, relay handoff/inbox, branch comparison, trusted checks, budget gates. - Closure path: isolated fan/relay alternatives → subsequent desk may compare or operator picks an outcome; a specific inter-operational oscillation feedback loop is not demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: branch isolation and passing completed results are valuable safety/hand-off mechanisms, but do not suffice for a separately accountable S2 coordinator. - Evidence: office modes and fan/relay semantics, office runner. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a user-authored desk prompt could instruct coordination, but that is not a function-specific first-party S2 agent loop in the inspected distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent-user whole-office task control over roles, execution shape, acceptance constraints and adoption of completed work in the current checkout. - Disturbance / variety regulated: differing desk work, unacceptable results, trust/cost limitations, conflicting output branches and risk of automatically committing an unaccepted candidate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the operator chooses which desks and modes run, supplies trusted checks and run limits, reviews the results/receipts/advisor outputs and selects a particular finished branch with metabot desks pick for staging into the real checkout. - Decision owner: the legitimate human operator of the local office; MetaBot&#39;s CLI/UI is the current-control actuator and desk agents are supervised operations, not independent whole-office priority owners. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: office.yaml, office check/trust/run, per-desk result statuses, signed office seal, local page and desks list/pick, deterministic checks/round limits. - Closure path: operator-visible whole-office run and competing branch outcomes → operator chooses desk scope/acceptance and final branch to pick → MetaBot runs the selected commitments or stages the chosen branch into the real worktree → next current operation builds on that outcome. - Boundary reachability: the installed MetaBot CLI documents and implements office run --desks/--fan/--until-checks and desks pick with local user authority; this is not inferred from OSS maintainer governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models produce each desk&#39;s work but the precommitted office shape and decisive choice of the actual branch for the current checkout remain the user&#39;s; machine checks cannot replace that discretionary adoption judgment. - Whole-system current view: the configured office, its selected desk/copy roster, run status, advice, checked receipts, candidate branches and completion offers shown through CLI/UI. - Current-control decision scope: task-wide resource/commitment choice (desks, fan, cost/rounds, trusted acceptance gates) and which candidate becomes current workspace work, not the repository owner&#39;s ultimate project identity. - Evidence: office user controls and pick semantics, office result/offer and runner, office command controls. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: P here is specific to an explicitly configured multi-desk office. Generic per-tool approval alone would not qualify; user control of whole current desk commitments and returned branch adoption establishes the bounded parent mode. No separately closed autonomous S3 office manager is claimed.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review an operating desk&#39;s actual code changes and return corrective advice to later desks and the office owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a build desk can produce incorrect, incomplete or overclaimed work that its own completion report fails to reveal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately launched, role-configured read-only advisor agent inspects the target desk&#39;s actual committed worktree/diff, judges what is wrong or missing and formulates advice about what to change. - Decision owner: the advisor&#39;s independent model/engine session, not the builder or receipt verifier; the operator decides whether to select the branch and later desks receive the advice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: advises mapping in office.yaml, detached read-only worktree, git diff with actual base, separate run/receipt, .metabot/inbox output and CLI advice display. - Closure path: builder&#39;s final commit/report → separate advisor opened on real builder worktree with raw diff access → advisor judgment written as a separate artifact → later desk receives the advice in its own inbox and operator sees it before picking results. - Boundary reachability: metabot office run supports first-party configured advisor desks and automatic post-build review; tests exercise the detached worktree, read-only protection and advice delivered to the next desk and user. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the advisor model, in a separate session, decides which defects matter; deterministic signed receipts, checks and worktree snapshots merely preserve evidence and transfer the findings. - Claim being audited: a completed builder desk&#39;s source changes and claimed task correctness. - Ordinary reporting path: the builder&#39;s own answer and branch/receipt. - Complementary access path: advisor&#39;s separate detached worktree at the builder&#39;s committed HEAD, with raw file reads and git diff against the builder&#39;s prior base. - Independence boundary: distinct desk role, fresh engine invocation and read-only worktree that cannot alter/commit the builder&#39;s code; it is launched after the target finishes rather than reusing the builder&#39;s claim as truth. - Who acts on findings: downstream desk receives the persisted advice in its work inbox; the human office owner sees the same advice while deciding which candidate to adopt. - Evidence: advisor prompt with raw diff and read-only requirement, advisor model configuration, test: advice delivered to later desk and person. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: advisor is an opt-in supported office configuration, not the default metabot ask; its advice is not a mandatory veto and its empirical correctness is not measured. Cryptographic receipt validity is not the S3 judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped future-environment sensing and operational-capability adaptation closure at the local task/office recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future engine availability, model quality and new tasks may require different capability, but configuration and fallback operate on the present user request. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate agent with a supported future-focused adaptation judgment and adoption path into the office&#39;s standing capability established. - Decision owner: operator for installed engines, office roles, routines and future configuration; ladder uses specified current-task success/failure conditions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model roster, current-task ladder/fallback, schedule-triggered routines, source plugins and operator config. - Closure path: no evidenced prospective outside/then intelligence → adaptation proposal → authorized changed current capability path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retries, model escalation when a test fails, scheduled goal execution and manual configuration do not themselves constitute strategic sensing and capability adaptation. - Evidence: ladder and routines, roster. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a human may revise future office configuration based on experience, but that separately governed prospective loop was not evidenced as a first-party task-cell function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy issue resolution closed at this local MetaBot office recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk of destructive tool calls, private data exposure and untrusted engines/skills calls for permissions, but these are operating policy constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor is shown adjudicating the ultimate identity/purpose of the office and returning authoritative policy; user approves/denies individual risk categories and sets fixed gate rules. - Decision owner: operator outside the operational agent cell for task permissions; local gate and signed journal enforce/capture provided authorizations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read/write/destructive gate, yes/no challenge, signing key, per-connection policies, audit receipt and trusted checks. - Closure path: operator-configured scope and per-call permission → enforced execute/refuse → signed report; no explicit identity-level dispute and returned whole-office governing policy path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts, user gates, receipts and code-of-conduct are not a runtime ultimate-policy body. - Evidence: gate/permissions and receipts, control mechanisms. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: separate deployed Metaorcha platform governance specifications and public OSS project policy are outside the selected MetaBot task-cell boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/caf5eca97c8b86a4515a655d65e651be50a809b4/assessments/me/metaorcha.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit caf5eca97c8b86a4515a655d65e651be50a809b4\">caf5eca &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T20:12:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Birfy/agentdescent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentDescent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Birfy/agentdescent/tree/d1cfa8f6eb570d927826278c70aa23b86d3c5264\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d1cfa8f6eb570d927826278c70aa23b86d3c5264\">d1cfa8f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce model-selected responses to heterogeneous tasks using the current candidate reusable skill/prompt artifact, and obtain outcome feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different task prompts and expected outputs, current skill weaknesses, uncertain model responses and live per-task result variance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The LLMAgent.solve model, reached by buildengine and eng.executor.rollout, produces a contextual answer from the rendered strategy and task; the scorer observes that answer and the same run may then change its candidate strategy through a separate reflection path. - Decision owner: Configured model through shipped LLMAgent and Completion adapter; the harness supplies the call route and feedback, whereas a test/demo&#39;s scripted offlineagent is not proof of model inference. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task and input formatting, scorer, workers/parallel task sampling, executor, candidate render, provider request/usage adapter and run/result logs. - Closure path: Active artifact snapshot + selected task → LLMAgent.solve complete → task response → scored outcome / recorded trajectory → subsequent task operation and separate prospective proposal if wrong. - Boundary reachability: The package exports LLMAgent and evolve, buildengine binds agent.solve to the rollout executor, and public documented examples use run=... / propose=reflector(model); the decisive modeled act is supported in shipped first-party composition, even though the selected provider is external. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model from the supported LLMAgent path leaves sampling, persistence and reward arithmetic but not contextual task-response synthesis. The model&#39;s answer is the S1 decision, not the merge gate&#39;s numerical acceptance. - Evidence: agentdescent/evolution.py#L120, agentdescent/evolution.py#L2250, agentdescent/evolution.py#L3740, agentdescent/agents.py#L1, README.md#L120. - Basis: Source-wired packaged model-backed operating path; no empirical inference performance attested. - Confidence: High for structural wiring, moderate for ability to run under the isolated evidence environment. - Caveats: The underlying task-solving model may itself be an application-supplied tool-using agent; its organizational metasystem and separate task services are not imported into AgentDescent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No positively evidenced organizational inter-S1 interference regulator with an autonomous ownership path and subsequent coordinated adjustments between independently viable S1 operating divisions is shown at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent rollout workers may propose contradictory edits against shared keys, race against stale ledger snapshots or exceed their task slice; these disturbances are candidate-artifact merge conflicts, not evidenced competing independent S1 divisional commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Default DefaultConflict.resolve chooses higher held-out-scoring contradicting diffs and DefaultFusion combines compatible ops; optional ReflectiveFusion can use a model to synthesize contested values. These are change-merger optimization decisions for one shared artifact, not separate mutual regulation of independently viable service departments. - Decision owner: Deterministic default conflict/verifier policy (or optionally a model behind pluggable fusion) governs candidate admission; no first-party organization-level S2 owner is demonstrated for independently operating S1 units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-worker snapshots, staleness filters, evidence buffer, DefaultConflict, DefaultFusion, optional ReflectiveFusion, section guards and serialized single-merger commit. - Closure path: Candidate Diff cards → conflict/merge rule → held-out gate → updated single artifact head → next worker snapshot; this is coherence of an experimental artifact, not sourced S1-vs-S1 interference/oscillation → S2 damping → coordinated separate S1 behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even model-assisted diff synthesis is not automatically an organizational S2 function; removing it leaves the same underlying single-artifact optimization goal rather than an autonomous coordinating relationship among distinct S1 departments. - Evidence: agentdescent/defaults.py#L90, agentdescent/defaults.py#L141, agentdescent/fusion.py#L127, agentdescent/aggregator.py#L827, agentdescent/evolution.py#L3686. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking both default and optional model-merger paths. - Confidence: Medium-high at the declared learning-harness recursion. - Caveats: If this framework is used to optimize several independently viable agents sharing operational resources, a new application-specific assessment boundary might expose real S2 evidence; generic N worker count does not prove it.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous executive management of a portfolio of independently viable present S1 operations with negotiated priorities/resources and binding feedback is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker errors, budgets, round timeouts, task allocation and token spending can destabilize an evolution run, but the linked responses are configured limits/scheduling and local algorithmic optimization, not enterprise-level management. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Caller sets workers, evaluation threshold, compute limits, task selection/policy and trust region. BudgetGovernor and worker health enforce/calculate thresholds; aggregator determines candidate fitness, not organizational present obligations across S1 divisions. - Decision owner: Operator configuration and deterministic package code; specific S1 model answers and S4 reflection are separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BudgetGovernor, sampler, WorkerHealth, max-worker-errors, timeout, token/governance/trust regions and commit policy. - Closure path: Resource/error readings → fixed or configured scheduling/budget/sampler actions → continue/stop/skip individual experiment; no first-party manager discretion over cross-S1 present capabilities and reallocation with an organizational return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Adaptive numeric compute placement and candidate selection are not a discretionary S3 authority over S1 commitments. - Evidence: agentdescent/evolution.py#L2795, agentdescent/evolution.py#L3515, agentdescent/budget.py#L1, agentdescent/pipeline.py#L49, agentdescent/aggregator.py#L1412. - Basis: Source-bounded negative across current-control and mode variants. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The human operator of a portfolio of deployment agents may be a real S3 externally; it is not a packaged parent-governed return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of S1/S3 operational reality with a mandate to correct a current S3 managerial picture and a returned management decision path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model-generated candidate edits could corrupt performance, hallucinate improvements or violate artifact invariants; the held-out/oracle check independently measures candidate success, but is principally a change-acceptance/quality gate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The ThreeLayerVerifier and AuditScheduler supply evidence and can veto/queue validation for prospective artifact changes. They do not inspect competing operational reports against underlying reality and return corrective findings to an established S3 owner in the assessed boundary. - Decision owner: Deterministic verifier/audit thresholds and external supplied reward/oracle; no separate first-party complementary auditor with governance corrective mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Held-out rewards, oracle audit queue, replay/diagnostics, immutable L0 guards, statistical selection and change acceptance. - Closure path: Candidate rollout evidence → held-out verifier/audit/acceptance decision → changed or refused future artifact; no independent operational inspection → correction of S3 current enterprise control → management action. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit terminology describes robust scientific evaluation for future learning, not necessarily VSM S3; model/reflection and a scorer can occupy S1/S4 feedback without a distinct operational executive audit relationship. - Evidence: agentdescent/verifier.py#L1, agentdescent/aggregator.py#L968, agentdescent/audit/wiring.py#L1, agentdescent/governance.py#L70. - Basis: Explicit audit/evaluation mechanics assessed under their concrete organizational closure role. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A deployment could adopt independent run-time compliance audits as an application function, requiring its own evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Learn from environment/task performance and explore future changes to the task-serving skill/prompt/harness, then adopt and feed those changes into the next operational version. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future task mix, repeated solver failure modes, reward changes, competition between candidate strategies and uncertainty about which generalized edits will improve upcoming task success. - External distinction: The worker runs the current strategy on sampled environmental tasks, records model outputs, measured reward and expected-result metadata, and uses those observations as evidence of current strategy inadequacy. - Future / prospective distinction: The LLMAgent.propose / reflector(complete) model reads the failed task, current rules, output and reward and proposes a generalized edit to the persistent future reusable artifact, not just a next-turn reply to the same task. - Adaptation option generated: The model chooses a candidate new instruction/rule/skill/harness value; Strategy.todiff produces a concrete prospective Diff, possibly alongside alternative proposals generated by peer workers. - Path back into current capability / S3: The aggregator scores/filters candidates with held-out evidence and invariant/governance constraints, atomically commits accepted Diff to the versioned ledger and the next rollout snapshots read the updated artifact. The return is into the operating solver&#39;s future capability, not into an independently evidenced S3 executive; absence of an S3 owner is not hidden. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Prospective option formulation belongs to the configured model through the shipped reflection adapter. The commit/adoption veto belongs to deterministic held-out/invariant gates and ultimately the operator-selected reward/constraints, which bound (but do not replace) model-generated strategic content. - Decision owner: Model-backed LLMAgent.propose or reflector in the supported shipped first-party evolve mode; deterministic gates authorize materialization, not conceptual option invention. Autonomous S4 is bounded to proposing and adopting prospective improvements under a predefined learning mission, not constitutional policy authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EvidenceCard, snapshot/ledger, selection and staleness, optional reflective model fusion, ThreeLayerVerifier, reward gate, budgets, stop conditions and transaction logs. - Closure path: External task-result contrast → model reflection/options → diff → held-out/invariant acceptance → committed persistent strategy/skill version → subsequent task solver uses new artifact → fresh feedback round. - Boundary reachability: LLMAgent.propose and public reflector(model) are shipped, buildengine actually wires them into evolve, proposeone calls propose(mine.render(),task,output,score), aggregator ingests card and commits to the same ledger used for next worker snapshots; public quickstart exercises this integrated model-and-artifact mode. The offline demo runs the same algorithm with a scripted surrogate, not claimed as model evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove the proposer model while leaving worker scheduler, scoring, merge and ledger intact and the general semantic rule/strategy revision would not be generated from task evidence; fixed gates can accept/refuse candidates but cannot themselves invent the task-specific future improvement. - Evidence: agentdescent/evolution.py#L120, agentdescent/evolution.py#L206, agentdescent/evolution.py#L2250, agentdescent/evolution.py#L3740, agentdescent/evolution.py#L3790, agentdescent/aggregator.py#L1412, agentdescent/ledger.py#L1. - Basis: Source-connected first-party LLM reflection and durable future version adoption; not evidence of production task-success gains. - Confidence: High for the option→accept→next-run wiring, medium for realized reward improvement. - Caveats: This is a bounded future operating-capability adaptation path; it does not imply independent monitoring of society, a full organizational S3/S5, constitutional delegation, or automatic improvement without configured model/reward/dataset.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate first-party agent/human constitutional authority is wired to receive fundamental purpose/identity conflicts and return binding enduring identity policy for the assessed organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dangerous self-modification and oracle/policy corruption are anticipated through frozen L0 and invariant guards; these are static protections rather than governance of identity conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator/developer sets the objective, evaluator, immutable artifact ids, blast radius and thresholds. assertmutable rejects frozen IDs and fixed policies enforce configured criteria; no bounded protocol resolves ultimate-purpose questions through a legitimate S5 authority. - Decision owner: External task/operator for constitutional objectives, deterministic gate for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: L0 frozen IDs, governance classify/immutable assertions, contract invariants, rewards, public configurable policies and audit gates. - Closure path: Configured constraint → proposed invalid mutation refused → continue or error; no question of organizational identity reaches a first-party S5 actor and returns as future binding identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Preventing a model from rewriting its own oracle is important security, but a hard-coded immutable condition cannot adjudicate the organization&#39;s ultimate purpose or legitimate authority. - Evidence: agentdescent/governance.py#L70, agentdescent/governance.py#L134, agentdescent/evolution.py#L2270, agentdescent/aggregator.py#L1426. - Basis: Bounded negative from both model-driven and governance paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External human owners may enforce or revise ultimate goals; the packaged learning engine contains no first-party return transaction qualifying as parent-governed S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c33e18ca6ab5f02cf7a5005434c9eefa821c06f7/assessments/ag/agentdescent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c33e18ca6ab5f02cf7a5005434c9eefa821c06f7\">c33e18c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T18:05:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/theStrangeAdventurer/capstan\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Capstan</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/theStrangeAdventurer/capstan/tree/60f284fc94ce96d05e72b52d2ffe55b4096b0177\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 60f284fc94ce96d05e72b52d2ffe55b4096b0177\">60f284f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement/inspect/verify a requested change through iterative model-selected actions in the project workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying user code requests, repository contents, tool failures, tests, model/tool availability, permission denials and returned outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next tool/action, revise approach from tool/test output, request delegation, and determine when the code change is complete. - Decision owner: Capstan&#39;s main model loop; child models own bounded delegated operational tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: C host, provider transport, Lua tool dispatcher, skills/profiles, sandbox and permit controls, sessions, guard and telemetry. - Closure path: prompt → model tool decision → permission-gated first-party action → returned observation → next model decision and correction or final response. - Boundary reachability: the shipped implement and headless/ACP modes call the same live agent runtime and expose the first-party coding tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: C executes and enforces; the model retains the task-specific operational discretion and reacts to evidence. - Evidence: runtime.lua, tools.lua, agent-loop.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external models/providers and approval rules are necessary dependencies/constraints, not additional credited autonomous owners.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party feedback loop specifically attenuating conflict or oscillation BETWEEN distinct S1 work units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel child tasks may contend for provider slots, tools and a shared workspace, but no demonstrated task-to-task interference regulator with feedback into subsequent individual S1 choices is shipped. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none meeting S2-specific closure at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child concurrency cap, inherited permission scope, per-child whitelist and turn limits, result ordering, deterministic queue, retry/cancellation. - Closure path: not established as S2; child results are returned to a parent after execution. - Boundary reachability: no positive path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a parent agent delegates tasks, while runtime queue and guard enforce chosen limits; neither demonstrates specific cross-child conflicting-operation detection and attenuation. - Evidence: subagents spec, subagent runtime, task store. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: concurrency-cap resource scheduling can prevent overload, but should not be re-labelled inter-S1 coordination absent disturbance-specific returned regulation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current-operation management loop with a current comprehensive view, substantive portfolio control and returned supervisory intervention is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: budgets, asynchronous child work and task-plan status are bounded locally; whole-current organization regulation is not demonstrated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identified management decision over all active S1 commitments beyond planning and routing individual work. - Decision owner: no S3 actor established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parent subagent orchestration, immutable ordered batch results, timeout/turn caps, local task statuses/revision, headless/TUI run status and cancellation. - Closure path: model sees completed child results and may launch more work; no live whole-current control or feedback path over active commitments is shown. - Boundary reachability: no positive path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model task selection and deterministic scheduling are not by themselves a whole-current authority. Human cancellation/permission popups likewise do not constitute a supervised current-control mode. - Evidence: subagents spec, tools.lua, tasks.lua, agent-control spec. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: genuine autonomous task-level routing is credited to S1, not automatically S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: shipped read-only child-model/tool compositions may furnish direct workspace access for a separate review judgment, but a complete independent complementary-audit/corrective-return path is not yet conclusively evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation mistakes and discrepancies between agent claims and actual files/tests could be examined, but the distinct review authority/return path needs confirmation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: potentially a read-only child-agent with fresh context evaluating artifacts; separate corrective-control ownership and actual triggering remain unresolved. - Decision owner: unresolved; the same-agent completion-review continuation is not independent, while an explicitly prompted subagent might make a first-party complementary audit possible. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: independent child Lua execution context, read-only profile/tools, fileread and shell inspection if allowed, model-selected delegation, and optional same-agent completion-review switch. - Closure path: potential parent initiates independent inspection → receives child findings → decides correction; no concrete built-in verification-trigger and return-to-action workflow was established by inspected sources. - Boundary reachability: a positive state is not claimed; child-agent read access and parent batch-result collection are shipped, but an audit-specific operational mode still requires function-level evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: mere generic delegation or the self-review continuation must not be treated as independent S3. The unresolved question is whether a concrete supported standard-mode parent-to-read-only-child audit has the required complementary authority and feedback. - Evidence: tools.lua, subagents spec, agent profiles, runtime completion-review implementation. - Basis: structural + unknown. - Confidence: low. - Caveats: requires source-first semantic adjudication of direct evidence access, independence boundary and real corrective return; no independently executed reviewer receipt is claimed here.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: in the opt-in shipped self-improvement mode, assess future repeat-use needs and construct a durable local agent capability in response. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new/repeating operator workflows and capability gaps whose solution must persist beyond one coding request. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select reusable command/model tool/hook shape, design a Lua plugin and optional skill, write it with first-party tools, and revise from validation/feedback. - Decision owner: the Capstan model when the owner has enabled the first-party self-improvement capability and authorized the relevant write. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configuration flag, embedded skill loader, ordinary permission checks, plugin scanner and hot reload, durable Lua plugin and skill directories. - Closure path: prospective workflow need detected in the user/environment → model designs persistent capability → permission-gated plugin/skill write → C hot reload registers future executable behavior → subsequent Capstan runs can invoke the new command/tool/hook. - Boundary reachability: the shipped C loader intentionally enables the bundled skill when capabilities.selfimprovement is true; shipped file-write/shell model tools can create the suggested durable plugin, and C watches/reloads it in the supported installation. This is an explicit opt-in first-party mode, not borrowed CI/dogfood or a hypothetical third-party plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the human controls whether the mode is enabled and grants file permissions, but the model determines the prospective adaptation content and implements the persistent future capability. The hot-reload loop enforces, rather than chooses, the change. - External distinction: prospective repeated workflow need and owner/project environment not yet served by present built-in command/tool set. - Future / prospective distinction: the requested extension should remain useful in later sessions and may serve later as a command, model tool, or hook. - Adaptation option generated: executable local Lua plugin, optional paired skill and automation hook. - Path back into current capability / S3: newly created plugin is hot-loaded into the active Capstan command/tool/hook registry for later agent use; no positive S3 is inferred. - Evidence: self-improvement skill, self-improvement spec, embedded skill loader, plugin reload. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: disabled by default; first-party agent may suggest an extension but cannot bypass the human&#39;s explicit capability and file permissions. This is not proof that Capstan automatically evolves itself without user-context needs or authorization. Independent semantic review should scrutinize whether the outside-and-then adaptation right is adequately instantiated in this opt-in mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate first-party ultimate organizational identity/purpose arbiter with returned constitutional-policy decision is established within the assessed session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission exceptions, selected agent profile, provider choice and extension capabilities are operational/configuration constraints, not demonstrated S5 identity reconciliation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for whole-system ultimate purpose/identity; owner configuration and permits set local limits. - Decision owner: no evidenced first-party S5 decision loop; ordinary parent user consent is not enough for a P state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lua configuration, profile selection, permit rules, static system prompt, first-party skill enablement and project AGENTS.md. - Closure path: user-selected execution permissions affect tools but no articulated identity/purpose matter traverses a legitimate ultimate-policy body and returns as authoritative organizational policy for future operation. - Boundary reachability: no positive path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves preset policy enforcement, not autonomous ultimate-purpose decisions; removing operator prompts removes configuration without revealing an autonomous S5 owner. - Evidence: permissions, profile policy, system prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: advanced externally authored governance policies are possible but not automatically part of this assessed distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2071d6aa11c9db2bf4b892eef326a369c19479eb/assessments/capstan.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2071d6aa11c9db2bf4b892eef326a369c19479eb\">2071d6a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T22:28:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ccxxxyy/mini-code-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mini-Code-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ccxxxyy/mini-code-agent/tree/ee8c4339b0dd04088377f0c27e04abc1bf9904b9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ee8c4339b0dd04088377f0c27e04abc1bf9904b9\">ee8c433 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute developer operations using model-decided reading, patching, shell, search, task and optionally delegated child-agent actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code/user goals, test and tool failures, permission denials, child/peer reports and varying repository state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each AgentLoop selects and revises substantive tool operations from observed results; child models own their bounded local operational decisions. - Decision owner: configured primary model and separately instantiated child models. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, async streaming, sandbox/permission manager, context compression, memory retrieval, event bus, tool result cache and differentiated child policies. - Closure path: task/context → model-selected tool calls → actual tool result/denial → feedback event/conversation → next model decision or completion. - Boundary reachability: Application constructs the primary loop and tool registry; SubAgentManager instantiates independent AgentLoop children for /spawn, model spawnagents and /team in shipped modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-backed loops select task-specific action and repair, unlike fixed enforcement and scheduling code. - Evidence: AgentLoop, SubAgent, application. - Basis: frozen executable source and mock-driven script tests. - Confidence: high for actual agent operating construction; not a live capability benchmark. - Caveats: provider calls and real model outcomes were not executed; one native subagent test hung under the local runner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate interference between concurrently deciding read/write child agents and dependent coding steps by model-selected isolated workspace and planned dependency boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel worker file collisions, premature execution before dependent research is complete and competing modifications of a shared repository tree. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the primary model selects the isolated option and delegated work topology through spawnagents; model-backed Planner selects per-step dependson/writesfiles, while the runtime enforces separate worktrees/read-only tool restrictions and dependency batches. - Decision owner: primary and planning models for coordination choices, deterministic SubAgentManager, AgentTeam and WorktreeManager for execution/enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-child Git worktrees, read-only child tool filtering, independent child task scheduling, dependency result gates and returned child/merge reports. - Closure path: potential overlap/dependency → model chooses isolation/ordered plan → independent S1s execute in bounded trees/batches → output/failure and dependency reports return to parent → subsequent task assignment can change. - Boundary reachability: shipped model tool spawnagents exposes isolated=true, which passes isolation=&#39;worktree&#39; to SubAgentManager; /team creates model-backed Planner plus team batches and passes dependency results to later workers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting isolation and cross-task dependencies is discretionary model judgment, while worktree creation, tool filtering and dependency checks enforce the selected relation. This is optional and not an unconditional guarantee of global conflict-free merging. - Evidence: spawn tool, team, planner, worktree. - Basis: direct frozen coordination paths and isolated mock/team/worktree tests. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded model-selected coordination choice and enforcement. - Caveats: defaults permit non-isolated children; dependency planning can make poor choices and Git branch integration remains a separate source of unresolved conflicts. - Distinct S1 units: independent model-backed SubAgent/pane workers running separate conversation/tool loops. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping edits in one repository tree and tasks attempted before prerequisites complete. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-selected worktree isolation plus planned dependency batches and automatic removal of write tools from read-only team members. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child results and prior-step outputs enter the next team prompt or primary spawnagents tool result; interference/error signals can change later work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: code explicitly changes the file-write collision topology and prevents dependency-violating/write-unauthorized peer execution rather than merely forwarding messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: provide the legitimate local operator a whole-current roster of spawned agent commitments and targeted intervention control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stuck, obsolete or competing-priority background children continuing to use finite time and tool/model resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an attending user invokes /spawn list, decides which active child should stop, and calls /spawn cancel &lt;id&gt; or cancel-all. - Decision owner: human operator as parent for the current session; fixed SubAgentManager.cancel implements the selected stop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listactive, getstatus, activesnapshots, current-agent progress board and cancel hooks propagated to child AgentLoop. - Closure path: whole-current list/phase → human chooses specific running agent → /spawn cancel &lt;id&gt; → SubAgentManager.cancel aborts its loop/task → changed active roster/remaining commitments. - Boundary reachability: Application wires the manager into first-party slash commands; /spawn list and /spawn cancel &lt;id&gt; are direct regular installed command handlers, independent of optional external governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the human operator decides a whole-current intervention, while the manager enforces it. The model-facing spawnagents can create children and receive results, but has no equivalent first-party current full-roster/selective-cancel tool; hence P, not A(P). - Evidence: slash commands, manager roster/cancel, progress UI. - Basis: first-party human current roster/action path and deterministic command tests. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded session control. - Caveats: human supervision is optional and requires attention; resource budgeting outside active child roster and independent installations is not proven. - Whole-system current view: /spawn list enumerates every active child id and phase in the current session; the board displays current child snapshots and progress. - Current-control decision scope: operator-selected cancellation of one or all current child agent commitments, changing ongoing work rather than merely gating an isolated file edit.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide a separate read-only model-run verifier to inspect a stated condition and issue an independent structured PASS/FAIL verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect or incomplete claims from normal coder output that ordinary model self-reporting or passing task actions can overlook. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an optional verify child independently reads code/runs read-only checks, calls syntheticoutput with verdict/failures and returns its report; the parent/operator is free to act on it. - Decision owner: separately instantiated verification model for its judgment; user/parent for optional invocation and follow-through. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct verify type prompt, restricted read-only tool list, isolated child conversation, syntheticoutput extraction and SubAgentResult.structuredoutput. - Closure path: user/parent selects verify child → separate source inspection and structured verdict → returned child output → possible parent/user correction. No mandatory or universally automatic correction gate is enforced. - Boundary reachability: agenttype=&#39;verify&#39; is a shipped spawnagents input and /spawn --type verify command option; SubAgent selects getagenttype policy/tools and extracts the structured result from its own transcript. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the auditor&#39;s model owns a distinct evidence-based verdict rather than merely formatting the coder&#39;s report, but independent audit→enforced current-control corrective closure needs explicit composition; therefore C rather than A. - Evidence: types/verify prompt, child tool/structured output, spawn tool. - Basis: directly exposed first-party verifier constructor, source-enforced tool restriction and offline type tests. - Confidence: medium for meaningful optional independent review construction, not reviewed model quality. - Caveats: reviewer is not necessarily institutionally/provider independent; a user-defined code-review skill or hash-chain audit log alone is not promoted to S3 and no mandatory audit-controlled repair was observed. - Claim being audited: condition or correctness claim about the working project and its implementation. - Ordinary reporting path: primary worker&#39;s own tool observations, edits/tests and completion summary. - Complementary access path: a fresh child conversation with read-only code/tool access and a verdict-specific synthetic output instruction. - Independence boundary: distinct child inference/context and enforced read-only policy; no organizational independence or separate provider required. - Who acts on findings: parent/model or human may initiate repair after receiving the returned report; no first-party mandatory veto/correction obligation is established.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no complete agent-owned prospective external sensing, option development and adopted future-capability return loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future tool/model/skill changes could require adaptation, but current mechanisms mainly provide session context, user configuration and task-specific retrieval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main agent can invoke installskill for a supplied local/Git source, but no shipped autonomous prospective adaptation choice or external/future scan is connected to this as an organizational loop. - Decision owner: operator or present-task model for explicit skill install and project preferences, fixed memory extraction/consolidation for session context retention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MemoryExtractor, MemoryConsolidator, skill registry, installskill, plugin loading and /skill commands. - Closure path: user/agent may install an existing skill or extract explicit user preferences into memory; no outside-and-future distinction → adaptation alternatives → first-party adoption judgment → changed continuing capability circuit is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: automatic extraction of user-stated preferences is not future capability selection; a generic model-callable skill installer does not alone own the prospective judgment about which external capabilities the organization should acquire. - Evidence: memory extractor, consolidation, skill install, skills. - Basis: inspected first-party durable-memory/skill paths and their authority boundary. - Confidence: medium for bounded negative; a stronger shipped prospective policy could change classification at a newer pinned ref. - Caveats: skill installation materially changes available runbooks, but source acceptance and requested installation alone are not sufficient evidence for a complete adaptive S4 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate identity/policy decision institution and returned governing loop is established for a local Mini-Code-Agent deployment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate purpose and legitimate cross-objective tradeoffs remain operator/developer choices external to the shipped current runtime governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator sets tactical permissions/model modes and developer rules; no separate highest-policy authority decides identity-level issues and governs later lower-system choices. - Decision owner: user for local tool permissions/identity hints and upstream maintainers for software design, not an installed S5 body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionManager, mode rules, OS sandbox, sensitive path restrictions, append-only hash-chain action audit and prompt instructions. - Closure path: action request → allow/ask/deny or audit record → task proceeds/fails; no identity-level policy issue → ultimate authority → return affecting organizational purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: recording tamper-evident tool actions or checking file permission is accountability/enforcement infrastructure, not deciding what the organization is ultimately for. - Evidence: permissions, audit log, app wiring. - Basis: policy/identity and audit runtime paths inspected. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the operator legitimately governs local work, but shipped controls do not form S5=P without the ultimate-policy loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c02cac9aa931535a6470913b0fd45adb1c3578cf/assessments/mi/mini-code-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c02cac9aa931535a6470913b0fd45adb1c3578cf\">c02cac9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-10T22:08:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/XYZ-AI-Lab/AxisAgentic\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AxisAgentic</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-10\">2026-11-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/XYZ-AI-Lab/AxisAgentic/tree/0aa54b14307942edc4401831110d8e7f726ec738\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0aa54b14307942edc4401831110d8e7f726ec738\">0aa54b1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: an agentic search/research operation transforms a user question and changing web/tool observations into an answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing or inconsistent online evidence, tool errors, large contexts, unexpected search responses and evolving draft answers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next research tool call, read observations, revise the query/answer and decide a final response within configured limits. - Decision owner: configured model-backed task agent operating through TaskOrchestrator and ConversationRuntime. The deterministic runtime limits but does not choose the research plan/content. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed message states, tool validation and dispatch, remote client, context compaction/rollback, retry caps and append-only trace. - Closure path: recipe task → model completion → structured search/scrape/code tool action → result returned to model-visible conversation → subsequent model decision or final answer. Optional failure handling can launch another bounded attempt. - Boundary reachability: shipped run configuration invokes the concrete recipe orchestrator derived from the model/tool runtime; it is not just an abstract SDK base class. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model discretion leaves fixed scheduling and tool/trace infrastructure but no choice of research questions/tools or answer. A provider is externally hosted; the first-party runtime nevertheless wires the executable model-to-tool feedback loop. - Evidence: runtime loop, conversation stage, tool manager. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for shipped agent-choice/feedback wiring; no real provider execution independently replicated. - Caveats: accuracy, successful external search credentials and longitudinal operational viability are not validated by static review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation with a returned coordination decision at the chosen task/recipe recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrently evaluated dataset tasks and nested tool calls may share compute or provider budgets, but they are independent evaluation jobs rather than interacting operational work cells in a sourced conflict-control loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identified coordination-specific discretion over clashes among distinct autonomous task S1 units. - Decision owner: developer-configured dataset concurrency and deterministic retry/tool execution; model agent decides only its own task/tool calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BatchEvaluator.maxconcurrent, task IDs, tool manager, nested OrchestratorTool and fixed attempt budgets. - Closure path: separate task results are logged and evaluated; no conflict signal from one S1 changes subsequent coordination behaviour of another S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an orchestrator name, concurrent sample scheduler and ability to nest an agent as a tool do not prove a concrete inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation/feedback relation. - Evidence: task orchestration, nested orchestrator tool, dataset evaluator. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the shipped recipes. - Caveats: a downstream user could implement genuine multi-agent coordination with these primitives, but it is not credited without the first-party path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no agent- or parent-owned whole-system current supervisory discretion over a portfolio of S1 resource/commitment choices is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: per-query failure/turn/context limits and batch completion are regulated with fixed configuration, not negotiated whole-current operational commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party supervisor selecting/revising current shared priorities, resources, accountability or interventions using a whole-operating-system view. - Decision owner: human/developer run parameters and deterministic orchestrator/batch scheduler; each agent controls only its local research task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model retry/backoff, timeout/turn/tool budgets, batch evaluator concurrency, benchmark dashboard and execution traces. - Closure path: local retry/budget responses remain within one S1 or evaluation job; no whole-current discretionary supervisory intervention feeds back into running S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hard budget enforcement and named orchestration do not own an S3 decision; no supported S3=P human decision-return mode is documented in the first-party recipe. - Evidence: runtime state, orchestrator, batch evaluator. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high in shipped modes. - Caveats: separate human-run experiment governance is not an operationally closed parent S3 mode of the assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: in the supported post-answer self-verification mode, an auxiliary model-backed verifier independently re-investigates the source question and challenges a candidate production answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: apparently plausible but unsupported/inaccurate research answers can survive ordinary production summaries and tool-use logs. - Claim being audited: the producing research agent&#39;s boxed/final answer to the original web-search question. - Ordinary reporting path: main WebSearchTaskOrchestrator returns its answer and writes model/tool trace; routine retry/format checks do not independently establish answer correctness. - Complementary access path: a separate auxiliary task ID and verification system prompt requests condition-by-condition fact checking with structured web-search tools; the verifier can collect its own observations instead of accepting only the author&#39;s written rationale. - Independence boundary: a new verifier conversation/task context and different verification objective/trace are established by runselfverificationauxiliaryattempt; its model endpoint and tool implementations may be shared, so this is task-context/evidence-search independence, not provider/model independence or a claim of adversarial human audit. - Who acts on findings: maybeapplyselfverification uses the verifier&#39;s correct/incorrect verdict to accept the answer or start a separate re-answer attempt; subsequent verdicts can change the returned result. Deterministic verdict parsing/attempt caps enforce the agent&#39;s judgment, not independently own it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge candidate answer against fresh research evidence as correct/incorrect; on rejection, cause re-answer rather than blindly return the original conclusion. - Decision owner: separate model-backed verifier operating within the first-party Web Search recipe, subject to static attempt/format limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated verification prompt/schema, auxiliary trace IDs, web tool manager, verdict parsing/resampling, bounded re-answer, exported trace metadata. - Closure path: original answer → auxiliary independent verification task with tools → model verdict → branch to acceptance or fresh re-answer → verified/revised result returned as the final task output and trace metadata. - Boundary reachability: the distributed Web Search default YAML sets selfverification.enabled: true; config runner passes this flag to benchmark runtime that constructs the dedicated verifier path. Generic class default False is explicitly overridden by this first-party recipe. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verification model evaluates the original answer using its own research session; fixed verdict parser and retry budget merely enforce its chosen judgment. This differs materially from post-run benchmark scoring or routine answer-format checking. - Evidence: self-verification prompt, separate auxiliary tool run, verdict and re-answer, enabled recipe config, CLI/runner wiring. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for a shipped separate context/tool-verification return path; actual independence strength and accuracy require empirical testing. - Caveats: verifier uses the candidate model; correlated model/search bias remains possible. The SelfVerificationConfig generic default is disabled and users can turn off the shipped recipe option; S3 is not claimed for every use of the reusable core library.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supported first-party prospective environmental assessment that selects a reusable future capability adaptation and returns it to current task operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing live web results are immediate research evidence, while trajectory export and evaluation can inform developer research outside the runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no source-wired runtime actor turns external/future changes into selected and deployed lasting changes to the agent&#39;s own operating repertoire. - Decision owner: developer/operator choices for model, recipes, tools, prompts and experiment setup; agents search to answer current questions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: search tools, context compress/discard, retries, trace/SFT export, evaluator and benchmark dashboard. - Closure path: web evidence changes an answer within S1 or an S3 verdict, not durable runtime future capability; post-run data exports are external research outputs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: search is outward-looking but task-immediate; trace learning/export is not a closed adaptation decision and implementation loop. - Evidence: architecture split, search recipe, SFT export. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at the shipped task-agent boundary. - Caveats: externally retraining a model on saved traces is possible but not credited as autonomous S4 here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate identity/policy authority with a governing decision-return loop at task-agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permissions, system prompts, benchmark constraints and token/turn budgets are preset execution boundaries, not constitutional disputes adjudicated in a runtime policy forum. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no evidenced agent or legitimate runtime parent accepting an identity-level issue and deciding an authoritative durable policy that governs subsequent operations. - Decision owner: external user/developer for YAML prompts, credential/tool/endpoint choices and benchmark/task policies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt templates, strict configuration schemas, tool managers, context/turn limits, sandbox restrictions and run stop rules. - Closure path: tool outputs and verifier judgments influence present answers, not ultimate identity/policy; no corresponding authority-return path for S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a static system prompt or nested orchestrator is not a policy-organizational owner; external experiment governance is outside the first-party task agent. - Evidence: config schema, runtime state limits, tool manager. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the shipped core/recipe paths. - Caveats: repository maintainers, benchmark owners and model providers can set external policy, but they are not granted a closed S5=P path by the runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2b84d5d2db11b7358e06f47c53742a805b56bdac/assessments/ax/axisagentic.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2b84d5d2db11b7358e06f47c53742a805b56bdac\">2b84d5d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:58:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QoderAI/better-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Better Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QoderAI/better-harness/tree/b605532fcf1014bcb48d3e6940a5bc64e80cddd5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b605532fcf1014bcb48d3e6940a5bc64e80cddd5\">b605532 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce evidence-backed diagnostic findings and, in a separate authorized route, a bounded repair outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: divergent project/session/asset evidence, uncertain provenance and differing workflow failure causes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: three specialist AI passes judge candidate findings in distinct lanes; the lead AI decides the final supported findings, severity, score and output narrative. - Decision owner: the host-executed AI specialists and lead, following the first-party packaged Skill. Host inference/tool delivery is support, not an independent Better Harness policy owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only evidence bundle, deterministic analyzers, scope limits, output schema, renderer and revision checks. - Closure path: evidence bundle → specialist results → lead reconciliation and report judgment → validated report and optional bounded repair result. - Boundary reachability: the published Skill explicitly directs host agents to run the three passes and lead reconciliation in ordinary installed host invocation; it is not merely a development example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: content judgment depends on AI discretion over findings and fixes, not on the JavaScript collectors alone. - Evidence: Skill Steps 1–4, README host routes. - Basis: explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: host inference and permissions must be configured; this is the installed Better Harness review work cell, not an independently booting agent runtime.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no established inter-S1 oscillation-regulation function at this review-cell boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no specific destructive cross-specialist interference is shown; specialists deliberately receive non-overlapping read-only evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none shown for a coordination decision that changes subsequent specialist behavior. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the Skill&#39;s parallel launch and evidence partitioning are scheduling/isolation, and lead reconciliation is terminal report judgment. - Closure path: no inter-S1 feedback loop beyond collecting independent answers into the lead report. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel execution and deduplicating similar findings do not establish a specific S2 disturbance-and-feedback relationship. - Evidence: independent evidence passes and restrictions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is not a claim that host coding agents can never coordinate in another product mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current whole-report control over the three evidence lanes and acceptance of their work on behalf of the diagnostic task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or inconsistent specialist evidence, unsupported claims, scope overruns and duplicate/conflated findings could undermine the whole review outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the lead AI accepts/defer/merges specialist findings, fixes final severity and scores, selects one support track, and stops or narrows publication on evidence failure. - Decision owner: the review&#39;s AI lead; validators enforce the prescribed result structure and the host executes commands. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundle lane status, quality gates, findings.json validation, renderer and hard cap on evidence specialists. - Closure path: cross-lane status and findings reach the lead → agent selects admissible current commitments and their priority → only reconciled findings are rendered or execution is stopped. - Boundary reachability: these lead rights are mandatory in Step 3 of the distributed Skill&#39;s normal analysis mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead chooses material accept/reject/priority judgments; code guards do not precompute which real findings deserve inclusion. - Whole-system current view: one bundle with three lane states plus lead facts, then all specialist candidates at reconciliation. - Current-control decision scope: acceptance, severity, support-track and report-level commitment—not control of the operator&#39;s unrelated coding tasks. - Evidence: Skill Steps 2–4, findings quality gate. - Basis: explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the whole-system boundary is the review cell; report acceptance should not be confused with general parent governance of all coding agents.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: a plausible independent post-fix check of a repair claim, but the complementary-access boundary is not fully established from the shipped instructions alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the repair-performing lead may overclaim that a reported finding has been fixed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a fresh read-only agent may judge verified, partial or blocked from the pre-fix finding, actual outputs, changed paths, native validation and refreshed integrity envelope. - Decision owner: a separately instantiated agent for the post-fix verdict, when supported; the deterministic recorder stores it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned finding lock, target topology binding, asset integrity collector and unchanged pre-fix baseline. - Closure path: independent verdict can update Repair Progress, while efficacy scores require later comparable episodes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an AI verdict exists, but the reviewer receives lead-provided bounded inputs, is forbidden to rescan the project, and can be omitted on delegation failure; it is unresolved whether its evidence access is materially complementary to ordinary repair reporting. - Evidence: finding-bound repair / Reassess the Repair Independently. - Basis: explicit for the route, unknown for independent complementary access. - Confidence: low. - Caveats: no independent S3 audit is inferred from ordinary specialist decomposition, validators or host subagent naming alone.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: prepare prospective improvements to the project-facing coding-agent workflow, with operator-controlled adoption of a selected repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving project needs, repeated session friction, missing rules/assets and changing operational evidence can make the existing workflow ineffective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the lead develops prioritized options; the parent operator selects and invokes an actual repair against an authorized project/finding, delimiting the adoption decision. - Decision owner: operator/parent for the adoption decision; AI lead for option formation and scoped repair implementation, with deterministic tools checking target/expected revision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/project/asset evidence collectors, repair callback, workspace topology and revision guards. - Closure path: observed project/session mismatches → agent-generated future improvements → operator invokes a finding-bound repair → host AI edits the authorized target → validators and recorded outputs inform later work. - Boundary reachability: both diagnostic findings with repair actions and callback-based bounded repair are first-party installed Skill routes; no external repository maintainer workflow is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent generates prospective options but the decisive commitment to change the live work environment is parent-selected in the evidenced finding-bound route. - External distinction: observed project changes, session outcomes and host agent assets rather than only an internal current subtask plan. - Future / prospective distinction: findings specify an improved workflow/asset and verifiable future operating outcome. - Adaptation option generated: bounded project/agent-asset repair and a prioritized expected output. - Path back into current capability / S3: applied local instructions/assets and recorded repair status influence subsequent host-agent sessions; later comparable task episodes, not same-run scores, measure effectiveness. - Evidence: README workflow and improvement route, Skill Step 3/5, finding-bound repair. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: reports alone are proposals; no automatic adoption, causal improvement or unattended future sensing is claimed. This P is a supported operator-selected adaptation path, not generic task approval.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no shipped operational identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop at this review-cell recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user-selected scope, risk and permission restrictions remain outer constraints, not an identified unresolved identity tension. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no such in-boundary right evidenced. - Decision owner: no agentic or first-party parent S5 closure established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Skill&#39;s fixed guardrails, stop conditions, permission scoping and CLI validation. - Closure path: no identity-level issue → legitimate parent decision → returned policy path is supplied; ordinary human choice of report scope or fix does not qualify. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static instructions constrain review actions without giving an actor an ultimate-policy decision process. - Evidence: Skill scope and guardrails, repair authority boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: product developers may make policy decisions in a separate repository-governance system, excluded from this installed review cell.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8671f5335902711f29462b3262914236a47f2cda/assessments/be/better-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8671f5335902711f29462b3262914236a47f2cda\">8671f53 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T21:07:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/risa-labs-inc/BossConsole\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BossConsole</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/risa-labs-inc/BossConsole/tree/d4db04882425327915e0520a366eac2da8af01f3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d4db04882425327915e0520a366eac2da8af01f3\">d4db048 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded software-engineering and other tool-mediated work in the relevant project/environment through an attached external AI coding-agent session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, project/repository state, terminal/browser/file feedback, tool results, model uncertainty, external services and task-local failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose reasoning, tool invocations and concrete task actions within the admitted objective and BOSS-enforced authority envelope. - Decision owner: the autonomous Claude Code, Codex, Gemini or OpenCode/model actor attached through the first-party BOSS terminal/MCP path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BossTerm, agent attachment/re-attachment, MCP registry, terminal/browser/editor/file/git tools, secrets, automation, RBAC/policy/approval and per-tool kill switches. - Closure path: objective is given to an attached coding agent → the agent reasons and chooses BOSS/MCP actions → BOSS transports/enforces those calls → project/tool/environment feedback returns to the agent → the agent continues until the bounded outcome is produced. - Boundary reachability: the pinned README documents launching the supported CLIs in BOSS terminals, attaching them to the local BOSS MCP server and automatically re-attaching them across restarts/port changes; this is a normal installed operating path rather than repository CI or dogfood infrastructure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: BOSS constrains and enables the work, but the substantive local task decisions remain with the external autonomous agent. Policy enforcement does not transfer S1 ownership to the deterministic host. - Evidence: README.md; docs/CLI.md; composeApp/src/commonMain/kotlin/ai/rever/boss/mcp/WorkspaceMcpToolProvider.kt. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the external agent runtime is credited only as the autonomous actor reached through BOSS&#39;s standard distribution; its internal harness functions are not inherited into BossConsole.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent destructive interference when multiple sibling coding-agent evolution sessions work on different changes to the same plugin repository in parallel. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more evolution agents operating in one source checkout can overwrite or mix one another&#39;s edits/branch state while independently changing the same plugin. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the isolation/coordination relation under which concurrent evolution work is separated into dedicated worktrees/branches rather than sharing one checkout. - Decision owner: the reviewed standard distribution does not package an autonomous S2 coordinator for this choice. The first-party Tool Evolver exposes an S2-specific worktree mode and allocator; an autonomous owner would still have to be composed over that path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EvolveMode.WORKTREE, ensureWorktree, dedicated evolve/&lt;slug&gt; branches, separate working directories, tracked evolution sessions and branch-specific generated skill guidance. - Closure path: several bounded evolution sessions target the same plugin → Worktree mode establishes a dedicated &lt;repo&gt;/.worktrees/&lt;slug&gt; checkout and evolve/&lt;slug&gt; branch per change → the selected CLI agent is launched with that isolated directory/branch → its subsequent file/git behaviour occurs in the separated worktree instead of clobbering sibling work. - Boundary reachability: the worktree mode is a shipped Tool Evolver UI/runtime path in the first-party companion explicitly integrated by BOSS; it is not a CI-only repository convention. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime mechanically creates and enforces the chosen worktree relation, but no autonomous actor in the standard path owns the decision to select that coordination mode. The primitive is nevertheless S2-specific because its own documentation states that it exists so several feature/issue evolutions for the same plugin can run in parallel without clobbering each other. - Evidence: EvolveLauncher.kt; EvolverTabViewModel.kt. - Basis: structural + explicit intent comment. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic JVM multithreading, multiple terminals, multiple attached agents and workspace plurality are not credited as S2. The positive mapping is limited to the concrete same-plugin parallel-evolution interference witness and its dedicated worktree relation. - Distinct S1 units: separate external coding-agent evolution sessions launched in separate BossTerm tabs, each responsible for a bounded feature/issue change to the same plugin. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent sessions sharing one plugin checkout can clobber one another&#39;s source/git state; EvolveLauncher explicitly defines worktree mode so several features/issues can be evolved in parallel without clobbering each other. - Attenuating coordination relation: one dedicated git worktree and evolve/&lt;slug&gt; branch per evolution session/change. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the selected coding agent is launched with the isolated worktree as its working directory and receives branch guidance requiring commits/PR work on that dedicated branch. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation is tied to a named concrete interference mode between sibling operational agents over the same mutable repository, and it changes their subsequent filesystem/git context specifically to prevent that interference.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate the running BOSS installation as a whole by observing current workspace/tool/plugin health and deciding which current workspaces, terminals, capabilities and agent tool rights remain active. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current workspaces can be active/stopped, plugins can fail or become unhealthy, MCP calls can be denied/failed, and tool access or current agent resource use may need intervention on behalf of the whole installation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current interventions such as opening/closing workspaces or terminals, enabling/disabling agent tools, setting current MCP policy/approval decisions, and acting on installation health/recovery findings. - Decision owner: base constructor mode — BOSS intentionally exposes structured whole-installation state and current-control operations to AI/automation clients, but does not package a distinct autonomous whole-system controller that owns those choices. Parent mode — the BOSS operator using the shipped workspace, Toolbox/MCP policy, kill-switch and health/recovery controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boss status --json, boss doctor, MCP registry/list/describe/invoke, WorkspaceMcpToolProvider, policy engine, approval UI, per-tool kill switch, recent MCP activity ledger, plugin watchdog and health/recovery surfaces. - Closure path: current installation/workspace/tool health and activity is observed → controller/operator chooses a present intervention → BOSS workspace/MCP/policy/plugin machinery applies it → exposed tools/workspaces/terminals or plugin state change → subsequent agent operation proceeds under the revised current state. - Boundary reachability: the CLI agent harness and workspace lifecycle MCP provider are packaged runtime interfaces; operator MCP policy/kill-switch and recovery controls are normal desktop surfaces. No maintainer or repository-development action is required for the claimed current-control paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: BOSS exposes enough function-specific state and authority for an autonomous controller to be composed, but ordinary attached coding agents are not documented as the installation&#39;s whole-system S3 owner. Deterministic policy/watchdog/lifecycle machinery enforces decisions rather than supplying that discretion. The operator mode does close the S3 decision and return path. - Evidence: docs/CLI.md; WorkspaceMcpToolProvider.kt; README.md; docs/release-notes/v9.5.19.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: individual approvals, process lifecycle, health checks and tool toggles are not promoted separately to S3; the positive mapping relies on their combination with installation-wide/current workspace/tool state and authority to change subsequent operation. - Whole-system current view: boss status --json/boss doctor report the running instance, active project and cross-area plugin/browser/MCP health; listworkspaces reports all existing workspaces with active/running status; the MCP registry and recent activity view expose the current governed tool plane and recent policy outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: open/create/close workspaces and terminals, change which tools an agent may call through policy/kill switches, and perform current plugin recovery/intervention based on health findings.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary organizational audit path is established for independently challenging an S1 producer&#39;s operational claim and returning findings into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: BOSS exposes health checks, activity/audit records, plugin signatures, security validation and Tool Evolver probe/verification, but these surfaces do not by themselves create an independent organizational challenge to ordinary S1 reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an independent audit judgment over an S1 claim. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boss doctor, health/watchdog reporting, MCP operation/activity ledgers, signature/integrity checks, policy validation, Tool Evolver memory/log probes and the evolve skill&#39;s self-verification step were inspected but are not credited as S3 ownership. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewed runtime supplies telemetry, deterministic integrity/security checks and producer-controlled/self-invoked verification, but no separate autonomous auditor with materially complementary access whose adverse finding is returned into current control. - Evidence: docs/CLI.md; README.md; evolve-skill-body.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository CI/Claude review workflows are adjacent development organization and excluded from the installed harness boundary. Tool Evolver verification is called by the same adaptation agent loop and therefore does not supply organizational independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: adapt the BOSS tool/capability repertoire when current or anticipated user/task/environment requirements expose a capability change worth implementing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing project/user requirements and tool behaviour can make an installed capability insufficient, faulty or suboptimal; the system must develop and test a revised capability for subsequent work without taking the running harness offline. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which installed plugin/capability should be evolved and what adaptation request should be pursued, then accept the resulting live-loaded change for continued use in the running BOSS instance. - Decision owner: autonomous base mode — an authorized attached agent can inspect available tools/probe evidence and invoke the first-party evolverevolve MCP tool itself with a selected plugin, agent and optional evolution task; the spawned coding agent owns implementation choices and applies/verifies the changed capability through evolverhotreload. Parent mode — the human operator selects the plugin, requested change and evolve action through the first-party Tool Evolver UI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evolverlisttools, evolverprobe, permission-gated evolverevolve, source-repository discovery/clone, generated evolve skill, build tooling, evolverhotreload, live plugin loader, probe/log verification, evolution session tracking and PR creation. - Closure path: external/user/task requirement or observed capability shortfall reaches an authorized agent/operator → a plugin and evolution task are selected → Tool Evolver launches a coding agent against the plugin source → the agent implements/builds → the resulting jar is hot-reloaded into the running BOSS instance → evolverprobe/logs verify the live capability and the agent iterates as needed → subsequent BOSS operation uses the changed tool; a PR is then opened for upstream persistence. - Boundary reachability: the pinned host README advertises Tool Evolver as a supported first-party Toolbox component and explicitly says an agent can reshape a tool while the app remains running; the pinned host contains the runtime Evolver MCP contract, and the contemporaneous first-party plugin exposes the agent-facing evolve/hot-reload tools used by that shipped path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unlike generic plugin hot-reload or self-improvement naming, the base mode gives an autonomous agent the actual adaptation decision/action path: select a target and task, launch evolution, implement, live-load, observe and iterate. Deterministic loader/build machinery supports that decision. A separate operator mode lets the parent own the adaptation request instead. - Evidence: README.md; EvolverContract.kt; Tool Evolver README; ToolEvolverMcpTools.kt; evolve-skill-body.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: internal memory/leak/log probing alone would not establish S4. The positive finding depends on the complete future-capability loop and on an external/user/task distinction supplying the adaptation need. The A claim is bounded to runtime capability of the installed BOSS instance; opening a PR does not give the agent ultimate authority over upstream maintainer merge/release governance. - External distinction: the adaptation request arises from user/project/task requirements in BOSS&#39;s environment, and Tool Evolver can acquire the target plugin&#39;s external source repository; the system is not merely consolidating internal memory or replaying a prior run. - Future / prospective distinction: the requested evolution is explicitly aimed at changing the tool so subsequent agent work has a different capability; the evolve loop tests the revised plugin live before upstream persistence. - Adaptation option generated: an authorized agent/operator selects a target and evolution task; Tool Evolver launches an AI coding agent that develops a concrete plugin modification on an evolution branch/worktree. - Path back into current capability / S3: evolverhotreload copies the newly built jar into the running host&#39;s plugin directory, unloads/loads it without restart, and the evolve skill probes/verifies the live result before subsequent operation continues.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure path is established at the running BOSS recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: BOSS has extensive RBAC, per-tool policy, approvals, secrets, role/admin semantics, plugin signing and capability-evolution controls, but these regulate operational authority, current control, security and adaptation rather than deciding what BOSS ultimately is or which identity-defining policy prevails. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: server-side RBAC/RLS, admin permission semantics, MCP allow/ask/deny policy, per-tool kill switch, secrets, signed plugins, operator approvals, plugin install/update/evolution and external upstream PR governance were inspected but are not promoted to S5. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no established S5 function to assign. Operator supremacy over ordinary permissions or capability changes is not itself identity closure, and the agent&#39;s ability to evolve tools remains S4 rather than ultimate policy. - Evidence: README.md; docs/release-notes/v9.5.19.md; docs/CLI.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong security/governance controls do not become S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy issue, legitimate ultimate authority for that issue and a return-to-operation path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f04815ff8e03993c24c7ad49d4093feb09462a74/assessments/bossconsole.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f04815ff8e03993c24c7ad49d4093feb09462a74\">f04815f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-23T00:15:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/marcelsud/claude-foreman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Foreman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/marcelsud/claude-foreman/tree/1863451a47f914a909f2b3857a73e87645ccd1fe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1863451a47f914a909f2b3857a73e87645ccd1fe\">1863451 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded implementation work in a target repository through a background Claude Code or Codex worker operating in a Foreman-created worktree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific repository state, implementation uncertainty, tests/build feedback, local tool outcomes, clarifying questions and review feedback from earlier attempts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive task-local reasoning path, edits and tool actions required to satisfy the assigned implementation prompt within Foreman&#39;s worktree/sandbox/approval envelope. - Decision owner: the autonomous external Claude/Codex worker actor launched through Foreman&#39;s first-party runner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/model/effort assignment, worker policy, worktree lifecycle, sandboxing, turn budget, exact approval hooks, cancellation, durable events and retry/requeue lifecycle. - Closure path: manager admits a task → detached scheduler claims it → Foreman prepares an isolated worktree and launches the selected external worker → worker makes task-local decisions and receives repository/tool feedback → worker reports completion/failure → Foreman runs verification and returns the result to metasystem review; requeue feedback can enter a later worker attempt. - Boundary reachability: background task creation and execution are the primary shipped product path documented in the repository README and developer reference; runner.py directly launches Claude/Codex worker integrations under the normal daemon workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party control plane owns admission, lifecycle and constraints, but the model actor owns substantive implementation reasoning. Foreman&#39;s deterministic scheduler, sandbox and policy hooks do not choose the code-level solution. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/README.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/runner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the internal organizational functions of Claude Code/Codex are not inherited. The positive state credits the reachable external operational actor only for the task-local decision right Foreman actually delegates to it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no installation-level S2 mutual-adjustment function is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foreman can run multiple tasks, isolate them in worktrees, sequence dependency-gated phases and keep a shared-worktree workflow history linear, but the inspected standard paths do not establish a concrete sibling-generated oscillation/interference signal together with a coordination decision/feedback loop whose function is to damp that disturbance between active S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-task worktrees, dependency records, atomic ready-task claiming, reviewed workflow phase gates, priority and worker-concurrency limits provide isolation/sequencing/resource structure without independently satisfying S2. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no qualifying S2 function was established, so there is no S2-specific decision right to classify. - Evidence: plugins/foreman/README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/workflow-schema.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: dependency scheduling can be part of S2 in another boundary if evidence ties it to a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. Here the documentation states dependency/prerequisite and linear-history semantics, not a distinct sensed sibling disturbance and corrective mutual-adjustment loop. Separate worktrees are preventive isolation rather than sufficient S2 evidence by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate the current Foreman organization by deciding task admission/configuration, routine approvals, responses to actionable worker events, review/requeue/accept outcomes and cancellation/goal state while preserving human authority over critical actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: queued/running/blocked tasks, approval requests, worker errors, failed verification, incomplete diffs, model/provider choices, dependency readiness, cancellations and risky actions can require whole-goal current intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — the autonomous managing Claude/Codex session chooses task definitions/configuration, routine exact approvals, monitoring responses, review feedback, requeue or acceptance; parent mode — the human user decides human-only critical approval requests and can direct/cancel/withhold acceptance through the manager interface. - Decision owner: base mode — the autonomous external managing assistant explicitly assigned the manager role by the shipped Foreman skill and MCP interface. Parent mode — the human user for critical actions and direct supervisory decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compact task/goal views, actionable taskwait wake events, durable event/approval state, exact hash-bound approval API, scheduler state, task configure/cancel, goal status, task diff, verification summary, requeue/accept transitions and daemon controls. - Closure path: Foreman exposes current goal/task/approval/verification state → managing assistant or human identifies a present-control issue → decision is made through first-party MCP controls → scheduler/runner/database changes current task/approval/goal state → durable events/wake surfaces return the new current state to the manager. - Boundary reachability: the root README instructs users to install the plugin into Claude Code/Codex and start a manager session; the shipped skill explicitly says the managing session is responsible for scope, approvals and final review, and mcpserver.py exposes the corresponding current-control tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the autonomous manager path is function-specific rather than inherited from generic model capability: the shipped skill instructs it to obtain an operational snapshot, wait on actionable events, decide scoped approvals, inspect diffs, requeue defects and accept complete work through Foreman&#39;s MCP tools. Deterministic scheduler/state transitions enforce decisions but do not own them. Human-only categories establish a distinct parent-governed mode. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/approval-policy.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/mcpserver.py; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: queue claiming, dependency release, wake IPC, turn budgets and lifecycle state machines are supporting mechanisms and are not counted as autonomous S3 merely because they supervise execution. The autonomous state is grounded in the shipped manager role plus reachable current-control tools. - Whole-system current view: tasklist/taskget, goal state, actionable event cursors, approval lists, verification summaries, usage and the dashboard expose current task status, worker/model, progress, approvals, dependencies, verification and worktree details across the Foreman-managed goal. - Current-control decision scope: create/configure/cancel tasks; set goal state; decide routine approvals; respond to worker questions; review full diffs/verification; requeue with feedback or accept; start/stop scheduler; escalate human-only risk.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently test the worker-produced repository state through declared verification gates and return that complementary evidence into review/rework before acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker can report completion while tests/lint/build/read-only Git checks fail, the verification environment errors, or the reviewed snapshot differs from what was actually tested. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define the verification criteria/commands whose independently executed results will challenge the worker&#39;s ordinary completion report before current control accepts or requeues the result. - Decision owner: Foreman does not package an autonomous auditor that chooses the audit criteria. The project/managing assistant supplies verificationcommands (or composes an explicit reviewer phase); Foreman supplies the dedicated independent execution, fingerprinting, persistence and feedback path. Therefore the S3 path is constructor-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate verification App Server process, argv-only command execution, workspace-write sandbox, network disabled, bounded output/timeout, worktree fingerprint, durable gate/result records, verifying state, actionable verification-failure events, full diff view and review/requeue/accept transitions. - Closure path: worker finishes and supplies its ordinary result summary → Foreman moves task to verifying → configured gates execute independently against the worker worktree and produce pass/fail/error plus tested-state fingerprint → task moves to awaitingreview with verification summary → manager inspects diff/evidence → failed/incomplete work can be taskrequeued with concrete feedback → Foreman persists review.feedback and injects it into the next worker prompt; accepted work closes the task. - Boundary reachability: verificationcommands are normal task/workflow inputs exposed by the shipped MCP surface and explicitly recommended by the manager skill; runner.py invokes runverificationgates on every finished task that has gates before review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verification service is organizationally separate from the worker self-report and its evidence is tied to the tested worktree snapshot, but the meaningful audit judgment is configured by the manager/project rather than selected by an autonomous first-party reviewer. The manager&#39;s later accept/requeue decision is S3 response to audit evidence, not transfer of S3 audit ownership. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/verification.py; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/runner.py; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/workflow-schema.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a reviewed workflow may explicitly add a separate Claude/Codex reviewer phase, but that phase is downstream-composed rather than a mandatory autonomous auditor supplied by Foreman. Its existence reinforces constructor capability rather than changing the standalone state to A. - Claim being audited: the implementation worker&#39;s claim that its finished worktree/result is ready to advance to acceptance. - Ordinary reporting path: external worker completes its task and returns a textual result/summary to runner.py. - Complementary access path: Foreman&#39;s separate verification service executes the configured repository commands directly against the finished worktree and computes a fingerprint of the tested tracked patch plus untracked files. - Independence boundary: verification runs through a distinct Codex App Server command process after worker execution, under its own sandbox/network/output/timeout rules; gate results do not depend on the worker&#39;s self-reported test summary. - Who acts on findings: the managing assistant receives actionable failed-verification/review state, inspects gate output/fingerprint and full diff, then accepts or requeues. Requeue feedback is persisted and returned to the subsequent worker attempt.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installation-level S4 outside-and-then adaptation loop is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foreman tracks durable event/usage history, provider/model availability, task outcomes and workflow versions, but the inspected paths do not establish environmental sensing with a prospective future distinction that generates an adaptation option and returns it into present capability/S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4 at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable SQLite history, usage summaries, live provider/model readiness checks, workflow versioning, task reconfiguration while queued and manager-authored workflow proposals. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operational history and current provider availability can inform a manager, but persistence/monitoring/configuration are not a packaged S4 environmental adaptation conversation by themselves. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/README.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can ask the external manager to research future options, but generic model capability or ad-hoc prompting is not a standard first-party S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no installation-level identity/ultimate-policy closure is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foreman contains strong safety policy, subscription-only authentication, exact approvals, human-only risk classes, immutable workflow review and explicit no-merge/no-deploy defaults, but these regulate operational authority and risk rather than organizational identity/ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval policy, humanconfirmed critical-action gate, worker policy, sandbox defaults, workflow activation review, model/provider selection and goal status. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human authority over destructive/external actions is substantial parent governance, but generic final say over operational risk does not satisfy S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy issue and return-to-operation closure at that level. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/approval-policy.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/workflow-schema.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/mcpserver.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the negative result does not mean Foreman lacks policy. It keeps safety/approval/workflow policy mapped to the operational functions they actually regulate rather than promoting them to S5 from vocabulary or human authority alone.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6d249c0ac2f32c3b180cdab2e30e55899d54ba9c/assessments/foreman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6d249c0ac2f32c3b180cdab2e30e55899d54ba9c\">6d249c0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:48:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Prism-Shadow/penguin-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PenguinHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Prism-Shadow/penguin-harness/tree/77c746954f92a588295de1c2dad362b1b8593f19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 77c746954f92a588295de1c2dad362b1b8593f19\">77c7469 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. A configured target agent autonomously performs task work through the Penguin runtime. Its model/tool loop owns the next operational action inside the configured task boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: —. Target, evaluator and optimizer are stages in an evaluation/optimization pipeline, not multiple operational S1 units whose mutual interference is regulated by an S2 function. Parallel evaluation cells and subagent calls are execution structure, not sufficient evidence of S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: —. The optimizer changes durable future Agent State after evaluations. That is not a separate whole-system current regulator with authority over shared organizational resources, commitments, priorities or constraints. The historical A promoted optimization authority into S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: A. The evaluator is deliberately separated from the target&#39;s normal production path: the target receives the public Statement but cannot access the private Rubric/Gold/scoring rules, while the evaluator later inspects the isolated workspace, bound root/child traces and the private Rubric to make its own score judgment. That score enters the optimizer&#39;s accept-or-rollback decision, changing subsequent retained Agent State. This establishes a first-party complementary audit channel with autonomous evaluator ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. The optimizer searches improvements against a frozen benchmark using scores and traces from the target&#39;s own task executions. Durable self-improvement is real, but the reviewed path does not establish the required outside-and-then environmental intelligence relation; optimization/learning alone is not S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. The target purpose, benchmark, desired score and optimization initiation are parent-authored constraints. They do not form a runtime identity/ultimate-policy tension-resolution loop with legitimate S5 authority. Parent configuration therefore does not imply P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529/assessments/penguin-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529\">911ebc9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T17:46:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KlaatAI/klaatcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Klaat Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KlaatAI/klaatcode/tree/b988be7cf6ffe7878e87ed3e720759e0f7247c33\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b988be7cf6ffe7878e87ed3e720759e0f7247c33\">b988be7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify a software project through model-selected coding/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation choices, tool/process/test failures, context pressure, permissions, provider/model variation and bounded delegated subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, decide whether to delegate, interpret returned evidence and determine when the task is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Klaat Code agent; write-capable build/general subagents own their bounded delegated work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, permissions/sandbox, diagnostics/verification, Plan/Build mode, context compaction, phase/cost guards, sessions, provider routing and hooks/plugins/MCP. - Closure path: user request → model selects direct action or delegated work → first-party runtime executes → results return to the model → model revises/validates or completes. - Boundary reachability: ordinary REPL/headless/server paths instantiate the first-party model/tool loop; \\delegatetask\\ is a built-in model-callable tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code constrains and transports execution, but the model owns open-ended engineering choices and interpretation. - Evidence: README.md; src/screens/repl.ts; src/tools/index.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: hosted Klaatu may perform additional routing, but no hidden hosted function is imported into this public-client assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent write-capable delegated agents can potentially overlap in one project workspace, but no implemented public-client mechanism is shown detecting/attenuating that peer interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persona tool scoping, permission prompts, atomic multi-file patch application and read-only explore/review personas constrain individual operations but do not coordinate peer write-capable S1s. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main agent may choose multiple delegated tasks, but delegation/concurrency is not itself coordination; roadmap worktree isolation cannot be credited as current runtime behavior. - Evidence: src/agent/personas.ts; src/screens/repl.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: read-only personas are intentionally parallel-safe; that prevents one class of interference by capability restriction but does not establish a general S2 loop for the write-capable operational units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control function is established over the live set of delegated operational commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: background child status is observable, but no standard actor has a substantive live control right over individual current child commitments beyond initial delegation and later result use. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: \\taskstatus\\, live output tails, completion notices, loop limits, phase/cost stops and whole-turn cancellation expose/enforce state without supplying selective live child reallocation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent agent can spawn and observe children but cannot kill, steer, retry or reprioritize a specific running background child through the reviewed tool surface. - Evidence: src/screens/repl.ts; src/tools/index.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: task visibility is necessary but insufficient; generic cost/loop stops and turn cancellation are enforcement/safeguards rather than whole-system current-control judgment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect a coding claim/change through a separate fresh read-only review agent and return findings to the caller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bugs, edge cases, security issues, needless complexity and defects missed by the authoring agent&#39;s ordinary coding path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect repository evidence independently and produce severity-ranked findings or a clean judgment. - Decision owner: the dedicated model-backed \\review\\ persona instantiated as a fresh delegated agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated subagent context, read-only tool allowlist, dedicated review system prompt, separate routing tier/loop cap and final-report return to the parent. - Closure path: main agent has a code/change claim → invokes \\delegatetask\\ with \\agent: &quot;review&quot;\\ and a self-contained target → fresh reviewer reads/searches repository evidence → reviewer returns findings → parent agent receives the final report and decides corrective/acceptance action. - Boundary reachability: \\review\\ is an explicit built-in persona and an enumerated value of the model-callable \\delegatetask\\ tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment is produced by a separate model context with direct repository-reading tools, not by deterministic linting or the author merely reviewing its own transcript. - Claim being audited: a specified implementation/change or coding claim supplied by the main agent to the review subagent. - Ordinary reporting path: the main/build agent&#39;s own tool results and completion statement. - Complementary access path: the review persona independently uses read/search/code-graph/web-read tools in a fresh context. - Independence boundary: isolated context, dedicated reviewer prompt, read-only capability boundary, no nested delegation and separate model loop. - Who acts on findings: the parent coding agent receives the reviewer final report and owns subsequent repair or acceptance. - Evidence: src/agent/personas.ts; src/tools/index.ts; src/screens/repl.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the slash \\/review\\ path is not needed for this claim; S3 rests on the dedicated fresh delegated reviewer with direct evidence access.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying changing external/future condition is put under an owner that develops adaptation options and returns them into current organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: automatic/session memory distillation, session resume, code graph, web tools, model switching, skills/plugins/MCP and update checks retain or extend capability but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory is distilled and injected into later sessions, but persistence/reuse of learned facts is not evidence of an external-and-prospective option-development loop returning into current S3 capability. - Evidence: src/agent/memory.ts; src/screens/repl.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: hosted Klaatu routing cannot donate an unobserved S4 function to the public client.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level policy issue is routed to an authoritative owner and returned as governing policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission rules, sandbox restrictions, project rules, system/persona prompts, config, hooks and Plan approval constrain execution without constituting identity governance. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coding/review agents operate within user/developer-authored rules and have no authority to redefine Klaat Code&#39;s ultimate identity or governing principles. - Evidence: src/permissions/index.ts; src/agent/personas.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user approval in Plan/permission flows is operational authority, not an identity/ultimate-policy decision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aeb6ed9e6d0d5caddf0fa3797327d1d0613d81af/assessments/klaat-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aeb6ed9e6d0d5caddf0fa3797327d1d0613d81af\">aeb6ed9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T23:22:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KhanUzeb/AXIOM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AXIOM</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KhanUzeb/AXIOM/tree/ba3a9bddce05990593035eab635eee9f45c46ff5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ba3a9bddce05990593035eab635eee9f45c46ff5\">ba3a9bd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist agents solve and revise the requested STEM or document-grounded task using calculations, code, research, retrieval and reasoning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user problems, unknown equations/constants, failed code, incomplete or inconsistent intermediate results and missing evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-backed coordinator selects work cells; model-backed specialists choose derivations, executable code, research/retrieval and follow-up work from task and returned results. - Decision owner: first-party wired model agents in the full graph; providers supply inference but do not choose the repository&#39;s organizational wiring, and deterministic tools execute selected acts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangGraph Send and reducer state, SymPy, code-exec timeout/static blocklist, Tavily, local RAG, provider fallback, memory and bounded hops. - Closure path: original query and intermediate observations → coordinator/specialist choice → computation or retrieval → returned result/critic suggestions → further specialist choice or assembled answer. - Boundary reachability: app/api/main.py constructs graph=buildgraph() and invokes graph.ainvoke/astreamevents for non-fast-path queries; buildgraph.py wires the specialists and return edge. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: first-party model calls choose actual solver/code/research work and adapt to tool results; deterministic school calculations are a separate bypass and are not credited as autonomous work. - Evidence: API graph invocation, graph wiring, coordinator, specialists. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source-wired agent rights; task success was not measured. - Caveats: provider and hosted tool dependencies were not invoked; the static code blocklist is explicitly not a hardened sandbox.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate actual shared-state write collisions and input-dependency conflicts among concurrently dispatched STEM specialist cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple Send branches write the same LangGraph superstep (InvalidUpdateError without reducers), can overwrite state, and code dispatched beside math/chemistry cannot see those same-hop prerequisites. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party mergedicts, keeplaststatus and narrow specialistpayload prevent update collisions; stageddependentagents schedules a bounded later code hop only after solver results become visible. - Decision owner: developer-defined deterministic S2-specific reducers/staging rules; model coordinator chooses possible parallel workers but does not own the decisive shared-write arbitration, so autonomous S2 authority is not established for this collision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangGraph reducer annotations, unique specialist result keys, one-shot staged-code marker and maxagenthops guard. - Closure path: concurrent specialist writes → reducer-preserved merged results → next coordinator state → dependent code is rescheduled with actual upstream result or task can aggregate; rejected duplicate work does not repeatedly collide. - Boundary reachability: the shipped graph uses GraphState and specialistpayload for every concurrent Send; the code dependency guard runs inside the standard coordinator path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this is more than generic routing because the source names concrete simultaneous-write failures and stale same-hop inputs, and implements attenuation with feedback; the decisive collision policy is first-party deterministic constructor machinery, not agent-selected negotiation. - Distinct S1 units: parallel math/physics, chemistry, reasoning, code, research, RAG and visualization specialists in a requested fan-out. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous writes to status/intermediateresults and dependent code lacking same-hop math/chemistry work product. - Attenuating coordination relation: reducer-backed per-specialist state merger, restricted Send payload and one-shot staged code dispatch on prerequisite availability. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: merged intermediate state determines the next coordinator route; the staged second-hop code agent consumes prior solver outputs. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: reducers and staged dispatch specifically prevent demonstrated parallel-write collisions and stale-prerequisite calculations, rather than merely forwarding tasks. - Evidence: reducer witness, minimal Send and stage routing, dependency-staging guard. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for collision/staging path; no measured multi-agent contention trace. - Caveats: last-wins scalar merging does not solve arbitrary semantic disagreement or concurrent external tool conflicts; no autonomous shared-resource negotiation is demonstrated.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separately established whole-system current resource/commitment regulation at this one-query graph boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: work cells may consume tokens, fail tools or repeatedly revise, but response is bounded by developer-set hops and fixed model/provider choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coordinator picks task specialists and critic-driven retries, while fixed hop caps can terminate; neither supplies evidenced shared resource bargaining, whole-current commitment control or accountability interventions across independent operations. - Decision owner: model for task decomposition and developer/operator for static limits; no distinct first-party whole-system S3 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MAXAGENTHOPS, provider fallback, step flags, graph termination and API status/streaming. - Closure path: current-task results and critic suggestions return to the same task loop, not to a whole-system resource-priority controller with return to multiple S1 commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming the entry node &quot;coordinator&quot; and providing hard limits is insufficient to establish S3; its view is of the one task, not a managed population of current commitments. - Evidence: coordinator routing, graph, settings. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped request boundary. - Caveats: external API deployment schedulers are not first-party S3 owners in this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a bounded class of specialist solution claims through a separate checking path before answer publication. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect algebra roots, unit/dimension mismatch, numeric/symbolic disagreement, chemistry mass imbalance and conflicting model-specialist accounts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate critic model evaluates original query, intermediate results, raw reasoning trace and programmatic rechecks, issues PASS/REVISE/FAIL and suggestions; failed independent programmatic checks override any critic PASS. - Decision owner: first-party wired critic agent for semantic audit judgment, with deterministic independent re-derivation and hard failure override; the solver specialists do not choose the critic&#39;s verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SymPy re-solving of original recorded equation, numeric cross-check, Pint dimensions, chemistry mass-balance routines, budgeted critic payload, verdict parser and graph gate. - Closure path: specialist claims/raw trace → independent re-derivation and critic judgment → criticreport → routefromcritic reopens specific specialist work (unless capped), or PASS allows aggregation; unresolved findings are surfaced in final answer. - Boundary reachability: buildgraph.py mandates criticnode before aggregation in the full graph; criticagent.py is a shipped node, not the optional offline evalharness. Fast school and /chat-doc bypasses are excluded from this positive mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a distinct model call judges cross-agent consistency and mistakes instead of merely accepting their output; separate deterministic algorithms rederive selected claims from lower-level evidence. Static override enforces the check but does not choose the critic&#39;s full audit findings. - Claim being audited: math/physics/chemistry correctness and consistency of specialist-generated answer components (not general correctness of arbitrary website facts or external provider output). - Ordinary reporting path: specialists write intermediateresults and summarized reasoning/answer paths for the aggregator. - Complementary access path: critic receives original query, raw per-specialist trace/tool inputs and a separately recalculated SymPy/numeric/unit/chemistry check, rather than only the aggregator&#39;s success assertion. - Independence boundary: distinct critic node/model invocation plus programmatic re-derivation independent of solver-produced numeric answers; both remain within one local graph/provider environment and trace inputs can still share upstream modeling errors. - Who acts on findings: the graph routes REVISE/FAIL into the coordinator&#39;s bounded rerun, and the aggregator reports unresolved faults at the hop limit. - Evidence: critic audit and verdict override, fresh algebra recheck and unit checks, returned critic control edge, unresolved handling. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium, restricted to corroborated classes of mathematical/structural claims; not observed audit effectiveness. - Caveats: a routine critic name alone would not qualify; the positive mapping depends on complementary raw-trace access and independent recomputation. The fallback may PASS when checks are unavailable, no independent external web-ground-truth audit exists, and no provider-backed run was executed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supported prospective environmental-model and capability-adaptation loop beyond present query solving. - Disturbance / variety regulated: research results, changing APIs and remembered user context can alter a current answer, but are not developed into future operating strategies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: research Tavily, PDF RAG and FAISS/Qdrant memory supply current-task evidence; developer/operator configures providers, model lists and future code. - Decision owner: task agents choose retrieval for the present problem, while maintainers/operators own durable capability changes outside the assessed graph. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tavily, RAG retrieval, saved turns, provider fallback, offline eval cases, static model/configuration. - Closure path: new facts or recalled turns feed the next answer, with no distinct prospective option selection returning to changed harness capability or S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: web search and persistence alone are S1 information gathering, not outside-and-then organizational adaptation. - Evidence: research node, memory façade, RAG. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at shipped service boundary. - Caveats: model providers can evolve externally, but that evolution is not a first-party AXIOM S4 decision owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate first-party ultimate identity/policy decision-and-return path in the shipped service. - Disturbance / variety regulated: credentials, code safety, allowed execution and API access are governed by operator/developer settings, not a runtime policy authority balancing identity decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator sets AXIOMAPIKEY, provider keys, hop/code limits; static blocklist/middleware enforces these inputs and agents solve within them. - Decision owner: external API deployer/developer for security and task constraints, not a first-party S5 autonomous or closed parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: API key middleware, MAXAGENTHOPS, static code blocklist, subprocess timeout, config/default prompts. - Closure path: startup rules gate requests/tools, without identity-level proposal → legitimate policy authority → authoritative decision → return governing subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: technical authorization and per-task acceptance/retry do not confer ultimate-policy authority. - Evidence: settings, API middleware, guardrails. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the shipped first-party service. - Caveats: public repository contribution decisions and operator-local admin choices are adjacent human governance, not in-run S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fb4819be4a410725dfe874e8613b665b53d7eee5/assessments/kh/khanuzeb-axiom.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fb4819be4a410725dfe874e8613b665b53d7eee5\">fb4819b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T16:00:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Grok Build</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build/tree/4247f661689354b831191f11eeeac8424993fe3d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4247f661689354b831191f11eeeac8424993fe3d\">4247f66 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-guided terminal coding actions against a real file/shell/VCS workspace, with live tool-result feedback and continued model turns. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requirements, changing code/build/test state, tool errors and provider response variability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and execute task-specific files, shell and code actions and adapt to tool results. - Decision owner: Hosted Grok Build root coding agent with model-guided tool call decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust xai-grok-shell agent runtime, model sampler, xai-grok-tools, VCS/workspace interfaces, permission and stop policies. - Closure path: User coding goal → model selects tool/action → tool effect → new observations → model continuation/finish. - Boundary reachability: Official terminal/headless/ACP grok modes activate the model/task/tool Rust agent from the packaged root shell and connect to first-party workspace tool operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party root uses actual task-conditioned model/tool execution; deterministic policy and tool runner restrict, but do not replace, the choice of coding actions. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/mvpagent/mod.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/mod.rs; README.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: ? - Function: Candidate coordination of concurrently operating subagent S1 units; no proven specific cross-child disturbance arbitration beyond quotas/queue and opt-in checkout separation. - Distinct S1 units: Independent child coding-agent tasks with separate subagent identifiers and optional worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Possible shared-workspace concurrent edits/collisions; source offers isolation but not a proven content-specific arbitration trigger. - Attenuating coordination relation: Bounded coordinator admission and optional worktree isolation are available, but not demonstrated as detecting a particular inter-S1 disturbance and returning corrective coordination. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Child completion/query and queue release inform the parent; a specific cross-child interference remedy is unproven. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: S2 not credited; the observed controls could be generic concurrency limits, session lifecycle and optional separation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Possible overlapping child changes to a shared workspace, exhaustion of concurrency slots, stale child messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Subagent spawn admission is bounded, and optional isolation=worktree alters child workspace; no definite interference-dependent S2 owner/feedback path located. - Decision owner: Coordinator actor and parent/root choice; function-specific S2 ownership unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Single-writer coordinator, Admission concurrency ceiling, pending/active queues, worktree option and separate child lifecycle. - Closure path: Child events pass through queued/active lifecycle, but no demonstrated inter-child content-conflict sensor and returned remedial assignment has been established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concurrency caps, independent child actors and worktree isolation alone are not evidence of a cross-S1, conflict-specific regulation loop. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/admission.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/mod.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Root coding agent controls current child task commitments, interrupts and collects returned subordinate results in its own team recursion. - Whole-system current view: Parent/root has child task list, pending/active/completed state and returned outputs in its own session&#39;s bounded team. - Current-control decision scope: Child task commitments, start/stop, future instructions and result integration within one coding-team run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Running, queued or stalled subagent tasks, completion/failure and changes to parent coding goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-root may invoke task spawn, inspect running children, await child outputs, send child messages and cancel/redirect child tasks. - Decision owner: Model-driven Grok Build parent/root agent using packaged task and subagent lifecycle tools; coordinator implements these decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task tool, coordinator registry, list/query/kill controls, active message delivery, root/child ownership enforcement. - Closure path: Child progress and completion → coordinator result/query to parent → root model selects further task/message/cancel or integrates child output → changed child activity. - Boundary reachability: First-party Task/subagent query/cancel/message interfaces are wired to the parent agent and coordinator and admit actual independent worker attempts rather than merely showing a static status dashboard. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model root chooses consequential team task commitments based on child outcomes, not merely reading one worker&#39;s progress bar. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/extensions/task.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/subagent/spawn.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator/query.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator.rs - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: No established independently sourced complementary audit of the producing coding agents or team current-operation claims. - Claim being audited: That the child or root-produced code actually fulfills its requested task. - Ordinary reporting path: A completed child returns a result into the parent Task/output and coordinator message path. - Complementary access path: Unresolved; neither cloud inline review comment records nor an optional reviewer persona prove separately observed source/evidence. - Independence boundary: A separately executing auditor with direct artifact sampling and corrective feedback is not established at the declared mode. - Who acts on findings: Parent may act on child output/messages, but no guaranteed complementary audit finding route was identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Erroneous developer work, unverified result claims and hidden defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate reviewer persona/agent can be requested as a child, but guaranteed independent direct-source observational audit and corrective return are not established as a shipped built-in closure. - Decision owner: Unresolved; review-comment extension stores UI events and is not an independent auditing agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subagent task/persona facilities; ACP review extension; standard tool feedback. - Closure path: Review comments/child messages may be consumed by root, but source-to-independent-reviewer audit → corrective control path is unproven. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A review UI event or model-provided reviewer persona is insufficient without materially independent observation and change in current work. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/extensions/review.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/backendtests.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Memory, task planning and persistent/long-running sessions are present, but no complete outside-and-then foresight and adaptation closure is established. - External distinction: Workspace and model inputs may refer to external trends or code, without an identified sensing-and-foresight role. - Future / prospective distinction: Persisted memory and long-running tasks affect later operations, but future time alone is not a prospective decision. - Adaptation option generated: Not established as a future organizational capability redesign choice. - Path back into current capability / S3: Memory/session resume can influence subsequent coding decisions; strategic capability-change closure remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed future user coding needs, project architecture and external developments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model can adapt a present coding plan and maintain memory, but capacity-changing future-oriented decision ownership is unresolved. - Decision owner: Unresolved between user/configuration and model decisions on immediate tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: xai-grok-memory, session resume, task tools and configuration. - Closure path: Saved context returns to future model tasks, without an established external prospective option/apply loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persistent memory or near-term task planning is not automatically VSM S4. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-memory/src/lib.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/mvpagent/mod.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/extensions/task.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Product identity, permissions and policy constrain operations but do not yet establish a function-specific ultimate-policy closure. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What Grok Build root/team is ultimately allowed to do and for whom. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: No confirmed ultimate-policy actor at this recursion; session credentials and user permission gates exist but do not qualify by themselves. - Return-to-operation path: Sandbox and permission decisions filter tool actions, without a demonstrated complete governing policy feedback path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User trust, sandbox boundaries, resource access and model policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runtime and human user authorize particular actions; no definitive highest-policy actor and feedback reorganization demonstrated. - Decision owner: Unresolved at project coding-team recursion; user permission enforcement is not enough to credit S5=P. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sandbox restrictions, workspace permissions, client authentication and configuration. - Closure path: Allowed/denied actions change execution, but an ultimate identity/policy choice returned across organizational operation is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Enforcing sandbox or workspace permissions is narrower than ultimate organizational policy authority. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-sandbox/src/lib.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/mvpagent/mod.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/rootcontrol.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e5406871ace31d3eb4a8b9394d2b0f5deebbafc0/assessments/grok-build.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e5406871ace31d3eb4a8b9394d2b0f5deebbafc0\">e540687 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T19:26:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kstawiski/evidence-gated-scientific-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Evidence Bench</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kstawiski/evidence-gated-scientific-agent/tree/85b396a824dfad6ec0880cbe07d86d3e24920ae8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 85b396a824dfad6ec0880cbe07d86d3e24920ae8\">85b396a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform research, produce a plan/report and generate grounded scientific evidence through actual model-directed source/tool interaction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain scientific question, varying source data and code results, inconsistent literature, missing provenance and model hallucinations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Qwen/Gemma planning models develop concrete alternative scientific plans, synthesizer selects a MasterPlan; first-party model researcher and reporter take bounded retrieval/computation/tool steps to produce a scientific report. Repair models use new observed findings to generate next revised outputs. - Decision owner: First-party ADK/model role logic invoked by runscientifictask; external inference is a configured backend, not an outside authored research operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan proposal/research/report prompts, ADK workflow, scientific ToolPolicy, retrieval evidence ledger, typed computation tools and deterministic lints. - Closure path: objective/inputs → independent model plan candidates and auditable master → model/tool research and computation observations → model report with cited outputs → critique feedback → model repair/new report. - Boundary reachability: The shipped orchestrator&#39;s runscientifictask calls buildplanningworkflow / runtyped, then producereport, which creates configured first-party ADK research/report toolsets. The same entry is reached through exported run and services. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model planners/researchers select substantive plan/evidence and responsive report/repair content from observed sources and tool results; static control and policy guardrails are separate. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/runtime.py; scientificagent/execution.py. - Basis: Reachable source-level model/tool/evidence-feedback implementation. - Confidence: High on installed loops, not measured scientific correctness or model inference. - Caveats: Actual Qwen/Gemma backend and any enabled optional code or MCP tools must be configured; benchmarking claims not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated control attenuating a material inter-S1 operational collision rather than merely collecting parallel proposals and sequencing task stages. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two planners may provide different suggestions, but they have no shared mutable working target or independently competing active operational commitment requiring stabilization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Qwen synthesizes proposals into one plan; deterministic serial stage/gates select what comes next. Neither path detects and resolves interference among independent ongoing S1 operations. - Decision owner: Planning join/synthesizer and single-run controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ADK maxconcurrency=2 parallel planner generation, JoinNode, typed candidate collection, and final deterministic lint. - Closure path: Two plan alternatives → synthesis → master plan, which is task content formation. There is no distinct operating-unit clash → anti-interference choice → changed sibling activity feedback documented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A comparison of concurrently generated plans, their disagreement and synthesis is not by itself a Beer S2 loop, nor do research budgets alone prove inter-S1 mutual correction. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/policy.py. - Basis: Bounded negative assessment of candidate planning/phase-control mechanisms. - Confidence: Medium: deliberate independent planning and synthesis is source-real, but no specific inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation contract was found. - Caveats: A deployment with multiple simultaneous runs may add coordination in a separate server layer; not credited without source proof for the assessed run.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Whole-current scientific run control: when plans are accepted, which research/evidence/critique phase may proceed, how scarce model/tool/repair attempts are budgeted and when final outputs are supported or withheld. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unreliable plans, unsupported evidence, unbounded tool/model calls, failing audit findings, code/tool nonprogress and bounded available repair resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: runscientifictask orchestrates planning/admissibility, reassigns rejected plans to repair, conditionally admits research, enforces research budgets, orders deterministic validation and independent reviewer, initiates limited repair, and sets final status rather than promoting rejected reports. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic Python controller and configured ResearchBudgetController, not autonomous scientific principal or another model owning organization-wide allocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planning.status, MaxRepairRounds, ToolPolicy, sandbox restrictions, deterministic lints, evidence/provenance ledger, needsrepair and finalrunstatus. - Closure path: Current plan/evidence/critic state and resource use → controller selects stop, execute next unit or bounded repair → new operations/results → updated audit+status → repeat or present final result/needs-human with recorded reasons. - Whole-system current view: Active master plan, model research state, evidence/retrieval and computation, deterministic validity, separate audit report, cumulative tool counts and attempts across one full scientific run. - Current-control decision scope: The constructor controls operational commitments of specialized planning, research/reporting, critique and revision operations, their admissibility and their shared run budget. - Boundary reachability: runscientifictask allocates ResearchBudgetController, executes prepareandauditplan, checks planning.status before producereport, validates outputs, invokes independent report review and loops repairs while needsrepair and the configured round cap hold; finalrunstatus controls final admission. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Decisive phase/budget/stop choices are expressed by static controller rules rather than a free model explicitly managing whole-current resource allocation; S3=C, not S3=A. - Evidence: scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/policy.py; scientificagent/config.py. - Basis: Source-attested current-commitment controller with feedback from multiple first-party operating roles. - Confidence: High for constructor current-run regulation; no external scientific-team budget governance claimed. - Caveats: Single-run scope is not project portfolio governance, and some phases use the same model provider for different prompts.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge active research plan and report claims from an alternative model/evidence path, with critic findings feeding real repair or an unresolved stop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsound scientific plans, citations without source support, fabricated computation values, contradictory data/display, incorrect visual readings and unsupported final conclusions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately instructed Gemma PLANAUDITOR audits the Qwen-generated MasterPlan; independent REPORTAUDITOR evaluates claimed results against retrieved/computed artifact evidence and deterministic validation, while visual review can inspect actual figure/table pixel inputs. Critic verdict/blocking findings can force a repair or prevent supported terminal status. - Decision owner: First-party distinct model-based Gemma critic invocation and structured VerificationReport judgment; constructor separately validates/reconciles the critic&#39;s outputs and commits control transitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan audit packet with independently generated checklist, retrieval/computation evidence, detached visual bytes, deterministic validators, unique audit artifacts and bounded repair envelopes. - Closure path: Qwen plan/report claim → independent Gemma plan/report/artifact review → structured blocking/nonblocking findings → controller&#39;s planningstatus or needsrepair chooses repair/withholding → revised plan/report and re-audit → supported/needs-human termination. - Claim being audited: Whether the plan&#39;s scientific method is admissible and whether report conclusions/displays are justified by gathered evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: Qwen research/reporter structured scientific report, planned steps and source/output claims. - Complementary access path: Gemma uses separately instructed audit prompts and controller-provided source/compute/citation/validation evidence plus actual rasterized displays, rather than merely accepting report narrative or self-score. - Independence boundary: Distinct model-critic role and alternative evidence/report read path; not a different OS trust domain, protected human team or globally independent provider. Strictly bounded to enabled first-party run configuration. - Who acts on findings: Constructor prepareandauditplan and report repair loop consume Gemma&#39;s verdict and findings; repairplan/ producereport receive them, final finalrunstatus refuses supported unless independent review is passing. - Boundary reachability: buildplanningworkflow invokes auditmasterplan on the actual master, and runscientifictask invokes auditreportresilient on the actual report/retrieval/computation. planningstatus, needsrepair and final status consume those outcomes to change installed execution, not merely save benchmark scores. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The alternative critical reading and structured falsification findings are selected by a model with separate audit instructions and evidence packet; deterministic controller owns repair scheduling/format admission but not the semantic critic judgment. S3=A, not just C. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/prompts.py; scientificagent/linting.py. - Basis: Source-reachable independent critic alternate evidence with actual correction-return and bounded blocking. - Confidence: High for model-based audit and repair wiring, medium for effectiveness of critic independence and coverage. - Caveats: On model outage or exhaustion the controller can return inconclusive/needs-human rather than supported. Model critic has its own error modes and deterministic checks sometimes reject contradictory critic instructions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No established prospective external strategic adaptation of the scientific organization&#39;s own future capability returned into whole-current S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Scientific literature discoveries, new input datasets and computational results are changing task environment, but are handled as evidence for the present user research question. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The agents retrieve literature and propose revised scientific methods, yet no first-party function evaluates externally changing opportunities or future organizational capabilities and installs a choice back into ongoing scientific-agent governance. - Decision owner: Model research/plan production and constructor task validation, not a separate future-capability adaptation function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Literature acquisition, knowledge library, revision tasks, plan synthesis/repair, model and tool configuration. - Closure path: External paper/data → current study evidence/plan → accepted report or within-task repair. No organizational environment foresight → comparative capability options → enduring adaptation choice → current S3 return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scientific research may explore future hypotheses, but that is S1 work for a requested scientific study, not automatic evidence of this harness changing its own future operational repertoire. - Evidence: scientificagent/literature.py; scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/knowledge.py. - Basis: Bounded negative review of strongest apparent prospective literature and revision flows. - Confidence: Medium-high within one-run recursion. - Caveats: External researchers/operators can improve prompts/tools in later repository revisions; those human activities are outside the reviewed deployed agent organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No owned ultimate policy/identity/ethos settlement path distinguished from fixed scientific integrity and tool safety rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Scientific risk labels, provenance obligations, tool permissions, domain/language constraints, evaluation failure and human reconsideration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Task requester and first-party configured controller impose a risk-sensitive scientific protocol and locked task constraints; on exhausted attempts the controller reports requireshumandecision, but it does not adjudicate ultimate organization identity/policy conflicts. - Decision owner: Human scientific owner outside runtime for ultimate risk and approval; fixed first-party admission rules for current-run integrity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MasterPlan/task protocol binding, ToolPolicy, static scientific safety rules, sandbox defaults, provenance manifest and risk classifications. - Closure path: Inputs/risk constraints → fixed scientific gate and possible human escalation. No first-party policy dispute → ultimate legitimate purpose/ethos decision → binding return to organizational governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fail-closed status, provenance hashes, immutable task locks and user escalation are robust current-run controls, not a system deciding its ultimate goals. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/policy.py; docs/MVPSCOPE.md. - Basis: Bounded negative review of policy/risk/identity surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A human scientific review board may have ultimate policy authority outside this product; no inherited S5 parent relation is scored without a first-party exposed closing loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/066ea03ff0b6e663b615313395c5ba10bf1c9f8a/assessments/evidence-bench.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 066ea03ff0b6e663b615313395c5ba10bf1c9f8a\">066ea03 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T22:09:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/whycorporation/whycodes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhyCodes</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/whycorporation/whycodes/tree/ef5126824739673d93815b29456ba3e5bdddf87c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ef5126824739673d93815b29456ba3e5bdddf87c\">ef51268 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: take discretionary local codebase engineering actions and repairs via first-party model-selected tools, and independently execute model-owned delegated subtask work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code state, compilation/test failures, incomplete edits, missing information and live tool error observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root or child coding model chooses its own repository read/edit/shell operations and responds to real outcomes. - Decision owner: active language-model agent within the standard runtime; a spawned general worker has its own model/tool context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: turn loop, tool/permission gate, local session and persistent history, code tools and optional subagent process/worktree. - Closure path: engineering request → model-selected tools → actual local execution/result → next tool/repair choice or completion. - Boundary reachability: normal whycodes agent loop and first-party registered task/swarm, not only tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed dispatch/sandbox and memory cannot choose a context-sensitive code change without the model&#39;s discretionary action. - Evidence: README.md; turn.rs; gate.rs; dispatch.rs; subagent.rs; mod.rs - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: concurrent first-party model-running general or other delegated coding workers in one swarm task wave; each has independent model turn/tool decisions and an assigned scoped goal. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two worker S1s can claim/write an overlapping source file in the same checkout, observe an out-of-date version of another worker&#39;s changed file, or contribute overlapping changes that would overwrite diverged main checkout content when merging isolated worktrees. - Attenuating coordination relation: FileClaimRegistry refuses double ownership, logs/reports stale reads and actively emits FileConflict; preclaims reject inconsistent paths before work; default separate worktrees quarantine writes and three-way guarded merge declines conflicting paths rather than silently clobbering main. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a conflicting same-checkout writer receives a tool denial/conflict owner, an attempted preclaim returns an immediate error to parent, and worktree conflicts appear in worker/aggregate results so the parent can narrow scopes or retry/reassign. Nonconflicting file changes are applied to the main checkout. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: these first-party checks regulate concrete multi-S1 interference over the same file&#39;s ownership or merged effects, not merely launch tasks or display messages. - Function: attenuate source-file/merge interference among multiple independently model-running coding S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent overlapping file writes, stale reads and conflicting post-worktree integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: deterministic same-checkout file-claim gate and three-way merge decide whether a conflicting write or integration may proceed; conflict event/result names the current owner/path. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party coordination constructor (C), not an autonomous S2 model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared registry, worker-specific context, read/write tool hooks, preclaim checks, worktree creation/merge, conflict events and status reports. - Closure path: independent workers approach same source path → exclusive claim or guarded merge compares ownership/base/main → unsafe action is blocked or marked conflicted → affected worker or root receives conflict information → subsequent work can adopt safe scoped action/repair. - Boundary reachability: types.rs default enables swarm/worktrees; gate.rs and dispatch.rs execute code-owned swarm entrypoint and claims/merge in supported modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: interference-specific admission/merge outcome is coded and remains if model decision-making is removed; the model may respond as S3 but does not own the fixed S2 conflict rule. - Evidence: swarm.rs; fileclaims.rs; swarmworktree.rs; dispatch.rs; subagent.rs; swarmhub.rs; modtests.rs - Basis: explicit first-party code paths and regression tests - Confidence: high for bounded technical write/merge conflict regulation - Caveats: same-checkout conflicts are observed only through claimed file operations; undeclared semantic conflicts and independent manually launched sessions are not guaranteed covered. Worktree conflicts can remain for manual/follow-on resolution.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: the root&#39;s swarm result aggregates every invoked worker&#39;s type, goal, completion/failure, duration, output, merge conflicts and applied file effects into one current-wave report; the parent retains the ongoing model conversation and tool outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: root model selects an initial set of worker roles, scope/path ownership and concurrency, then can elect an additional/corrective wave, change active task scope assignments for the next wave, or directly repair integration issues based on aggregate current worker status/conflicts. - Function: manage the current set of delegated coding commitments through model-selected work partitioning and evidence-based follow-on assignment/correction between waves. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uneven worker completion, failed tasks, merge conflicts, integration tradeoffs and current coding workload allocation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root model selects which present work to assign to which subordinate type and scope, and how to revise current remaining work after observing whole-wave results. - Decision owner: top-level model over the supported first-party swarm mode, not fixed semaphore/worktree merge code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: swarm task spec/tool contract, aggregate report with per-member status/conflicts, model turn tool-result return, and S2 guarded application. - Closure path: model assigns a concurrent worker roster → independent workers act and current report returns including failures/conflicts → model can choose changed worker brief/scope, follow-up task or repair → next first-party tool/run applies the revised commitment. - Boundary reachability: default-enabled first-party swarm in actual Agent::runturn execution, not an unwired utility or example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic worker scheduling and reporting cannot determine appropriate future current-job assignments or repairs absent the root model&#39;s discretion. - Evidence: swarm.rs; swarm.rs; dispatch.rs; gate.rs; turn.rs; types.rs; subagent.rs - Basis: structural, with supported tool-model response loop - Confidence: medium for wave-boundary model-control closure, no live provider session run - Caveats: manager sees consolidated wave results after workers settle; real-time mid-wave organizational reprioritization/preemption and whole-deployment resource policy are not claimed. This is current multi-worker job management, not prospective S4.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped separately owned complementary reviewer established to independently inspect an implementing S1&#39;s current completion claim and return a corrective verdict to the first-party S3 current manager. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect reported code completion or regressions accepted without a separate direct-evidence challenge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent reviewer verdict/return path within assessed standard modes. A swarm report describes worker outcomes; file merge check detects structural conflicts but not independent semantic quality of coding claims. - Decision owner: implementing coding model and the root can run ordinary tests/read code; optional human/CI review remains outside system. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktree guarded merge, file-claim conflict reports, generic read-only scout/explore workers, permission and test tools. - Closure path: results are reported to parent coding model; no first-party dedicated independent reviewer claim check → manager judgment → required corrective operation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic worker read-only capability or worktree merge safety does not become a separate source-first complementary auditor by labeling output an audit. - Evidence: dispatch.rs; swarm.rs; subagent.rs; swarmworktree.rs; README.md; mod.rs - Basis: structural review of shipped roles/wiring -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future-oriented environmental intelligence and applied organizational capability adaptation owner demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future coding environments, model/provider capability shifts and new classes of tooling. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no model or legitimate parent selects organizational future capabilities then returns an implemented change. - Decision owner: user/config/developers set provider/workflow/skills; root handles present coding work and current worker allocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory, session compaction, skill registry, provider routing, SDK/daemon interface, schedule and contextual tools. - Closure path: configuration and memory inform current actions, but future environmental sensing → strategic capability selection → applied new operating capacity is not wired as one ownership cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adding workers to the current coding task or recalling previous files is S1/S3 present control, not S4 organizational adaptation. - Evidence: README.md; architecture.md; lib.rs; mod.rs; types.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate organizational mission/identity policy authority with binding corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: choice over organizational purpose/constitutional priorities, beyond acceptance of individual code edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no legitimate S5 owner sets/reconciles final mission values and returns binding governing change to subordinate organizational functions. - Decision owner: operator/project owners configure prompts, tool capabilities and permissions; model selects execution within the declared scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission policies and user approvals, tool/shell sandbox, session roles, inherited worker restrictions. - Closure path: risky tool actions allowed, denied or paused under user/developer rules; neither constitutes an organizational identity-policy choice nor changes the system&#39;s governing purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: per-tool allowed/denied and sandbox boundaries enforce action safety, not ultimate organization policy. - Evidence: types.rs; mod.rs; gate.rs; subagent.rs; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/20907c5be09105accc3e1e28053abcab39b2f98d/assessments/whycodes.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 20907c5be09105accc3e1e28053abcab39b2f98d\">20907c5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T19:45:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/omergocmen/CrewCtl\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CrewCtl</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/omergocmen/CrewCtl/tree/834c399cf28a9e83e7e47f81544ed52dfbeb92cc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 834c399cf28a9e83e7e47f81544ed52dfbeb92cc\">834c399 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out an assigned code or research task through model-directed inspection, editing/tool use, testing and result reporting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repository files, build/test feedback, failed tool calls, task errors and unexpected implementation constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The specialist LLM CLI chooses task-specific implementation and verification actions inside the delegated work; it returns a result/error for subsequent rounds. - Decision owner: Configured coding CLI specialist invoked from the first-party runAssignmentWithRecovery / runCli path, under the assignment&#39;s role and limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subprocess adapter catalog, working-directory sandbox settings, timeouts, retry/failover, task store, optional worktrees and verification commands. - Closure path: Operator-selected assignment → specialist model/tool execution against code → output and observable files → stored state.results → new operator/team context. - Boundary reachability: The installed crewctl engine&#39;s normal runTask path invokes the configured agent executable via runAssignments and runAssignmentWithRecovery, using its returned output in the same live task; fake CLI fixtures are not needed to establish the shipped wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model-driven coding specialist selects actions. CrewCtl orchestrates and constrains them; the externally installed CLI itself is a required runtime dependency and is not incorrectly claimed as CrewCtl source code. - Evidence: engine runAssignments, runCli and runTask, supported adapters, executor role. - Basis: explicit first-party operational wiring; no live external model test. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Installed provider/account capability and task success cannot be inferred from source or mocked CLI tests.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent operational cross-contamination among simultaneous task teams operating on one source repository. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independently running task teams can overwrite each other&#39;s files, reuse working state or misattribute status events when they share a directory and process identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The first-party task-level mode constructs separate worker engines and assigns each task a distinct Git worktree/branch and correctly tagged event stream; the configuration/user still selects this mode and no autonomous inter-team conflict arbiter is evidenced. - Decision owner: The concrete isolation and queue-claiming primitive is owned by CrewCtl&#39;s supervisor/worker and worktree runtime; no separate S2 agentic decision owner is closed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxConcurrentTasks, per-worker engine slots, task claiming/skip IDs, worktree creation from HEAD, task-indexed messages and event routing. - Closure path: Concurrent queue items → separate task worker and worktree identity → each specialist reads/writes only its team&#39;s checkout → results committed to separate local branches and events routed to the correct task; any higher-level conflict resolution between output branches is not supplied. - Boundary reachability: The shipped supervisor accepts maxConcurrentTasks &gt; 1 and worktree.mode: task through documented config and invokes worktree.create in the live task path; the native concurrency test exercises two simultaneous team runs, separate checkout paths and correctly associated events. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is a specific first-party S2 construction and isolation path, not an autonomous agent evaluating conflicts and changing pairwise coordination; hence C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: Two concurrent per-task specialist coding-agent teams, each with its own operator, task state and repository worktree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Shared-file modifications, workspace contamination, queue double-claim and cross-task event attribution. - Attenuating coordination relation: The supervisor claims tasks once and routes workers into distinct worktrees/branches with per-task event IDs. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each team&#39;s next CLI run executes in its own checkout and consumes its own task-specific results instead of the other team&#39;s dirty files or events. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The dedicated worktree/multi-worker mode directly attenuates a concrete interference between independent active task teams, although it stops short of automatic merge/conflict arbitration. - Evidence: worktree runtime, engine concurrent worker slots and task checkout, native concurrency test, opt-in mode defaults. - Basis: first-party source and test-defined interference case. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Disabled by default; C is narrower than resource-priority coordination or agentic resolution of conflicting changes across branches. User must enable concurrency and task worktrees.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Control the active task team against its current objective, evidence, limits and interruptions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous task decomposition, failed delegated work, unavailable agents, red verification gates, incomplete delivery and excessive review rounds. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The operator LLM makes structured assignment/dependency decisions and subsequent continue vs complete/replan decisions from returned current-state reports; deterministic guards constrain or occasionally bypass its judgment. - Decision owner: Configured operator-model CLI with CrewCtl&#39;s first-party plan/review prompts and validation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: normalizeAssignments, role-chain completion, dependency runner, failure feedback, progress state, evidence/diff snapshots, verify gate, completion-proof and budget guards. - Closure path: Task and whole-team context → model-selected assignments → execution/reviewer/verifier evidence → operator model&#39;s next decision → new assignments or guarded task completion. - Boundary reachability: Normal runTask invokes invokeOperatorJson on the planning and review phases using operatorPrompt; decisions reach runAssignments and complete in the same task process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator chooses priorities, specialists, remedial work and task completion from team state. The built-in queue, dependency ordering and hard verification constraints execute rather than originate that plan. - Whole-system current view: Current task prompt, project context, enabled team capabilities, prior assignments/results/messages, verification evidence and resource/round status appear in the operator prompts and state. - Current-control decision scope: Select task-work distribution, delegate or repair blocked work, re-plan remaining commitments, decide completion within the configured guardrails. - Evidence: engine operatorPrompt, runTask, runAssignments, runVerifyGate, operator role. - Basis: explicit first-party model decision and closed task loop. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Fast mode intentionally elides some second-stage operator review; no parent modifier is inferred from manual task creation, start/stop or risky-plan approval.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Challenge implementation reports with direct code/test inspection and return an independent quality finding into task control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A specialist can report success while files are unchanged, acceptance criteria are missing, or a claimed test was not executed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A review-specialist prompt requests first-hand filesystem/test observations and VERDICT: PASS|FAIL; the engine parses the verdict and passes it to operator decision/completion guards. - Decision owner: The configured external review-capable CLI model when a reviewer is assigned; the default role contract establishes the auditor-specific path but does not guarantee distinct reviewer identity or universal enforced read-only rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer.md inspection rubric, kind: review delegation, dependency ordering after implementation, extractVerdict, latest-review checks and delivery-proof safeguards. - Closure path: Implementation reports/files → separately invoked reviewer model inspecting files and tests → parsed verdict and findings → operator receives review message and replans, or gated PASS allows completion. - Boundary reachability: In shipped balanced mode, ensureBalancedRoleChain automatically adds a review assignment if a compatible agent exists; specialistPrompt loads the packaged reviewer role and the live runAssignments path parses the result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer LLM would choose the finding, but independence is not enforced when the same configured agent is eligible for implementation and review, and the read-only rule is partly prompt-based; therefore the first-party complementary audit constructor is credited as C, without asserting universal closed independent A audit. - Claim being audited: Implementer statement that work meets the task&#39;s acceptance criteria, changes exist and verification evidence is sufficient. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementation agent output and task completion report to the operator. - Complementary access path: Reviewer is instructed to inspect changed files, call sites, tests and diff evidence directly, independently of the implementation report; configured read-only MCP route is optional. - Independence boundary: Separate review delegation/role and verdict, but a distinct underlying CLI identity and truly enforced filesystem read-only access are not mandatory in default configuration. - Who acts on findings: Operator LLM through its review-phase decision and the first-party completion/verification guard. - Evidence: reviewer role, balanced role chain and review handling, MCP routing. - Basis: function-specific first-party audit path; independence gap bounds state at C. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Reviewer availability depends on installed/configured CLI agents; prompt-only review discipline is not proof of technical read-only confinement.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated agent-owned prospective adaptation portfolio/option selection returning into the organization&#39;s current capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing project facts and provider availability are potential environmental changes; CrewCtl handles them as current task execution context or deterministic resilience. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate S4 option-development and prospective adaptation right evidenced. - Decision owner: Not established within the credited runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Post-success operator-authored CONTEXT.md revision, per-task memory, scheduled tasks, CLI discovery and provider retry/failover. - Closure path: Context refresh may inform the next task, but no evidenced external/future distinction → adaptation-option judgment → governance/current-capability change closes S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM maintenance of facts about work already completed is useful memory, not by itself environmental scanning and prospective adaptation ownership. - Evidence: engine reviseProjectContext and resilience, scheduling. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Developers could add an adaptive strategy, but such a possible extension is not first-party evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is established for the local CrewCtl team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risky plans, permissions and unsafe tooling affect ordinary task execution but are not proven existential identity or organization-purpose decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No authoritative identity/policy revision decision and returned organizational change evidenced. - Decision owner: User selects goals and grants initial autonomous consent; ordinary per-plan approval does not establish S5 authority at this assessed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: autonomousConsentAcceptedAt, approvalMode, risky keyword patterns, sandbox mode, MCP role gating, CLI overrides and resource budgets. - Closure path: Human ask/approve may unblock a task, but not an identity-level issue → legitimate policy judgment → governing return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Neither the operator&#39;s task plans nor the human&#39;s runtime consent decides what the CrewCtl organization ultimately is or ought to become. - Evidence: engine consent and plan approval, default config, MCP role filters. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External owner and maintainer governance may determine ultimate project identity, but repository presence of those actors does not establish this function&#39;s returned first-party path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/756e81aee3272ab7002ba760f6c668c318185784/assessments/cr/crewctl.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 756e81aee3272ab7002ba760f6c668c318185784\">756e81a &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
